        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Check If Candidate Attachment is Present in Input Payload"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "true": "1777976535797",
            "false": "1775136169284"
          },
          "code": "CHECK_IF_CANDIDATE_ATTACHMENT_IS_PRESENT_IN_INPUT_PAYLOAD",
          "convergenceTargetId": "1769152320477",
          "id": "1769152174975",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1769152174986_1",
              "name": "condition",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "{{$context.$nodes.CHECK_IF_CANDIDATE_ATTACHMENT_HAS_VALID_DATA.$output.result}}"
            }
          ],
          "type": "CONDITION"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Get Candidate Attachment Information"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1769152237106"
          },
          "code": "GET_CANDIDATE_ATTACHMENT_INFORMATION",
          "id": "1769152208207",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1769152208211_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "You are an AI assistant that analyzes one candidate referral email and one resume attachment, then returns a JSON payload for a job search API.\n\nThis is a deterministic extraction task.\nUse only the provided input.\nDo not hallucinate, infer unsupported facts, enrich with external knowledge, or use data from outside the input.\n\nThe API uses JobSearchKeywords to search jobs.\nJobSearchKeywords must be accurate, concise, deterministic, and based only on the provided input.\n\nCandidates may belong to any business function or industry.\nDo not assume software, technology, engineering, sales, finance, healthcare, or any other function unless explicitly supported by the input.\n\n---------------------------------------\nINPUT\n---------------------------------------\n\nYou will receive:\n- Email subject\n- Email body/content\n- One resume attachment containing:\n  - fileName\n  - mimeType\n  - context\n\nInput:\n{{$context.$nodes.ATTACHMENT_CODE_BLOCK.$output.result}}\n\n---------------------------------------\nALLOWED SOURCES\n---------------------------------------\n\nUse ONLY:\n- Email subject\n- Email body/content\n- Attachment fileName\n- Attachment mimeType\n- Attachment context\n\nIgnore completely:\n- contentBase64\n- encoded content\n- binary content\n\nDo NOT decode or analyze contentBase64.\nDo NOT use external knowledge for candidate facts, skills, employers, education, experience, title, certifications, seniority, or location.\nDo NOT infer missing candidate details.\nDo NOT use OM search results or returned job data.\n\n---------------------------------------\nOUTPUT RULES\n---------------------------------------\n\nReturn raw JSON only.\n\nDo NOT:\n- Use markdown\n- Add wrappers\n- Add explanations\n- Add comments\n- Add extra text\n- Omit any key\n\nReturn exactly one valid JSON object:\n\n{\n  \"Title\": \"\",\n  \"FileName\": \"\",\n  \"Description\": \"\",\n  \"JobSearchKeywords\": \"\",\n  \"SpecificJobNumber\": \"\",\n  \"CandidateLocation\": \"\",\n  \"CandidateTitle\": null,\n  \"SeniorityLevel\": \"\"\n}\n\nAll values must be JSON strings except CandidateTitle, which may be a JSON string or null.\n\nThe response must start with { and end with }.\n\nSeniorityLevel is mandatory and must always be exactly one of:\n- FRESHER\n- JUNIOR\n- INTERMEDIATE\n- SENIOR\n\nNo additional keys are allowed.\n\n---------------------------------------\nSPECIFIC JOB NUMBER RULE\n---------------------------------------\n\nExtract SpecificJobNumber only from the email subject or email body/content.\n\nPopulate SpecificJobNumber only when the employee explicitly asks to refer the candidate to a specific job, requisition, posting, opening, vacancy, or job reference.\n\nDo not treat generic plural phrases such as:\n- jobs\n- roles\n- openings\n- positions\n- opportunities\n\nas a specific job identifier.\n\nValid trigger labels include:\n- Job ID\n- Job number\n- Job code\n- Job #\n- Requisition ID\n- Requisition number\n- Req ID\n- Req #\n- Posting ID\n- Opening ID\n- Vacancy ID\n- Job reference\n\nExamples:\n\n\"Please refer this candidate to job - 12993\"\n-> SpecificJobNumber: \"12993\"\n\n\"Req ID ABC-123\"\n-> SpecificJobNumber: \"ABC-123\"\n\n\"Referral for Job Number 456789\"\n-> SpecificJobNumber: \"456789\"\n\n\"Refer the candidate to job ZBEN_001AAA\"\n-> SpecificJobNumber: \"ZBEN_001AAA\"\n\n\"Refer attached candidate to USA jobs\"\n-> SpecificJobNumber: \"\"\n\n\"Refer candidate to United States jobs\"\n-> SpecificJobNumber: \"\"\n\n\"Refer candidate to QA jobs\"\n-> SpecificJobNumber: \"\"\n\nFor SpecificJobNumber, remove only surrounding label words and obvious separators, such as:\n- job\n- req\n- requisition\n- posting\n- opening\n- vacancy\n- ID\n- number\n- code\n- reference\n- refer\n- candidate\n- :\n- -\n- quotes\n\nPreserve the actual identifier value exactly, including letters, numbers, underscores, and internal hyphens.\n\nIf multiple specific job identifiers are explicitly requested, return a comma-separated string preserving first occurrence order.\n\nDo NOT mistake any of these for a job identifier:\n- phone numbers\n- dates\n- years\n- salaries\n- employee IDs\n- document IDs\n- resume IDs\n- attachment IDs\n- file IDs\n- experience years\n- graduation years\n- certification years\n\nIf no specific job identifier is explicitly requested, return \"\".\n\n---------------------------------------\nCRITICAL JOB ID RULE - TERMINAL FOR KEYWORDS\n---------------------------------------\n\nBefore applying any other JobSearchKeywords logic, check SpecificJobNumber.\n\nIf SpecificJobNumber is not empty:\n- Set JobSearchKeywords to exactly the same value as SpecificJobNumber.\n- Do NOT include resume skills.\n- Do NOT include resume title.\n- Do NOT include CandidateLocation.\n- Do NOT include employee-requested location.\n- Do NOT include state.\n- Do NOT include country.\n- Do NOT include seniority.\n- Do NOT include functional area.\n- Do NOT include domain.\n- Do NOT include industry.\n- Do NOT include tools.\n- Do NOT include technologies.\n- Do NOT include certifications.\n\nThis terminal rule applies only to JobSearchKeywords.\n\nOther output fields may still be populated if they can be extracted directly and safely from allowed sources.\n\n---------------------------------------\nTITLE RULES\n---------------------------------------\n\nFor Title:\n- Extract only the candidate's most recent/current job title if explicitly identifiable.\n- Prefer titles associated with current, present, working as, current company, latest experience, or most recent experience.\n- Ignore historical titles unless no current title exists and the historical title is clearly the most recent role.\n- Ignore internship titles, training titles, certification titles, project titles, and education titles.\n- Do NOT derive Title from fileName.\n- Title is NOT the candidate name.\n- If current/recent title cannot be determined confidently, return \"\".\n\nExamples:\n\nResume says:\n\"Cognizant | Jan 2025 – Present | Associate – Performance Engineering\"\n-> Title: \"Associate – Performance Engineering\"\n\nResume says:\n\"Professional Summary: Performance Test Engineer with 5+ years...\"\nand current role says:\n\"Associate – Performance Engineering\"\n-> Title: \"Associate – Performance Engineering\"\n\nIf no current/recent title is explicitly identifiable:\n-> Title: \"\"\n\n---------------------------------------\nFILENAME RULES\n---------------------------------------\n\nFor FileName:\n- Copy attachment fileName exactly as provided.\n- Do NOT change casing, spacing, spelling, punctuation, or extension.\n- Do NOT reconstruct FileName from candidate name or title.\n- If fileName is missing, return \"\".\n\n---------------------------------------\nDESCRIPTION RULES\n---------------------------------------\n\nFor Description:\n- Generate a concise professional summary strictly from attachment context.\n- Use email content only for explicit candidate facts.\n- Do NOT include email instructions or referral instructions.\n\nInclude only explicitly present information:\n- Current/recent title\n- Functional area\n- Skills\n- Tools\n- Technologies\n- Platforms\n- Domain\n- Industry\n- Certifications\n- CandidateLocation only when it is explicitly current/residing/work/based/located or employee-requested\n\nDo NOT include:\n- Permanent address\n- Family address\n- Native place\n- Hometown\n- End-of-resume address\n- Referral instructions\n- Requested job locations unless they are relevant as CandidateLocation\n\nOnly include location/address details when explicitly marked or clearly expressed as current, present, residing, working, located, or based.\n\nIf insufficient information exists, return \"\".\n\n---------------------------------------\nCANDIDATE LOCATION RULES\n---------------------------------------\n\nCandidateLocation must represent either:\n1. The target job location positively requested by the employee, OR\n2. The candidate's current/present/residing/based/work location from the resume when no employee-requested location exists.\n\nCandidateLocation must NOT represent:\n- job locations returned by OM search\n- majority geography of returned jobs\n- recruiter location\n- employer location unless explicitly stated as the candidate's current/work location\n- phone number country code\n- email domain\n- project rollout geography\n- client geography\n- company headquarters\n- global product geography\n\nPriority 1: Email-requested job location.\n\nUse email subject and email body/content first.\n\nPopulate CandidateLocation when the employee positively requests a target job location.\n\nExamples:\n\n\"Refer the candidate to United States jobs\"\n-> CandidateLocation: \"United States\"\n\n\"Refer the candidate to US jobs\"\n-> CandidateLocation: \"United States\"\n\n\"Refer the candidate to USA jobs\"\n-> CandidateLocation: \"United States\"\n\n\"Refer attached Candidate to USA Jobs\"\n-> CandidateLocation: \"United States\"\n\n\"Refer the candidate to CA jobs\"\n-> CandidateLocation: \"CA, United States\"\n\n\"Refer the candidate to California jobs\"\n-> CandidateLocation: \"CA, United States\"\n\n\"Refer the candidate to Pleasanton jobs\"\n-> CandidateLocation: \"Pleasanton, CA, United States\"\n\n\"Refer the candidate to CA, Plesanton jobs\"\n-> CandidateLocation: \"Pleasanton, CA, United States\"\n\n\"Refer the candidate to CA, Plesanton, USA jobs\"\n-> CandidateLocation: \"Pleasanton, CA, United States\"\n\nNormalize only these explicitly supported examples:\n- US, U.S., USA, U.S.A., United States of America -> United States\n- CA or California, when clearly used as a United States job location -> CA, United States\n- Pleasanton or Plesanton, when clearly used as a job location -> Pleasanton, CA, United States\n\nDo NOT normalize or expand other ambiguous locations unless directly supported by the input and highly certain.\n\nExclusion handling:\nIf the email subject or body/content says not to use, exclude, avoid, not interested in, or do not refer to a location, do NOT set that excluded location as CandidateLocation.\n\nExamples:\n\n\"Do not refer the candidate to India jobs\"\n-> Do NOT set CandidateLocation to India.\n\n\"Exclude Hyderabad jobs\"\n-> Do NOT set CandidateLocation to Hyderabad.\n\nIf the same email contains both included and excluded locations:\n- Use only the included location that is not excluded.\n- Do not use excluded locations as CandidateLocation.\n\nPriority 2: Candidate current location from attachment context.\n\nUse attachment context only when no positive email-requested location exists.\n\nFrom attachment context, populate CandidateLocation only from the candidate's explicit current, present, residing, based, located, or work location.\n\nValid signals include:\n- Location\n- Current Location\n- Candidate Location\n- Present Location\n- Based In\n- Residing In\n- Work Location\n- Currently in\n- Located in\n- Address, only when clearly current/present/residing/work location\n- Contact/header location, only when it appears as the candidate’s current contact location and there is no conflicting location\n\nExamples:\n\nResume header:\n\"email@example.com | +91 99999 99999 | Kochi, India\"\nand no employee-requested location exists\n-> CandidateLocation may be \"Kochi, India\"\n\nResume says:\n\"Current Location: Hyderabad, India\"\n-> CandidateLocation: \"Hyderabad, India\"\n\nResume says:\n\"Based in Bangalore\"\n-> CandidateLocation: \"Bangalore\"\n\nIf multiple locations exist in the attachment:\n- Choose the one most clearly marked as current/present/residing/based/work location.\n- If multiple locations remain ambiguous after applying those labels, return \"\".\n\nDo NOT use permanent, native, family, hometown, or end-of-resume address details unless explicitly stated as the candidate's current, present, residing, based, located, or work location.\n\nThe following MUST NEVER become CandidateLocation:\n- phone numbers\n- email domains\n- company names\n- employer names\n- technologies\n- project descriptions\n- banking references\n- recruiter locations\n- OM query text\n- OM search results\n- returned jobs\n- returned job locations\n- majority geography of jobs\n- global platform references\n- project rollout countries\n- client regions\n- organization geography\n- APAC references\n- EMEA references\n- multi-country references\n- supported countries\n- global banking platform references\n\nInvalid examples:\n- +91 does not mean India.\n- adcdat05.us.oracle.com does not mean United States.\n- DBS Bank does not mean Singapore.\n- Java does not mean USA.\n- AWS does not mean United States.\n- United States jobs returned by OM does not mean CandidateLocation is United States.\n- APAC rollout does not mean Singapore.\n- Global banking platform does not mean United States.\n- Most returned jobs are US jobs does not mean CandidateLocation is United States.\n\nIf no valid CandidateLocation can be extracted, return \"\".\n\n---------------------------------------\nCANDIDATE TITLE RULES\n---------------------------------------\n\nFor CandidateTitle:\n- Use only attachment context.\n- If the candidate is clearly a fresher, return \"FRESHER\".\n- If the candidate has professional experience and an explicit current/recent title, return the actual title exactly as stated.\n- If the current/recent title cannot be retrieved and the candidate is not clearly a fresher, return null.\n\nTreat a candidate as FRESHER only when the attachment explicitly indicates one of:\n- fresher\n- fresh graduate\n- recent graduate with no professional experience\n- 0 years experience\n- no work experience\n- entry-level candidate with no professional experience\n\nDo NOT treat internships, academic projects, trainings, certifications, or education titles as professional CandidateTitle unless the attachment explicitly states a current/recent job title.\n\nExamples:\n\nResume says:\n\"Cognizant | Jan 2025 – Present | Associate – Performance Engineering\"\n-> CandidateTitle: \"Associate – Performance Engineering\"\n\nResume says:\n\"Professional Summary: Performance Test Engineer with 5+ years...\"\nand current role says:\n\"Associate – Performance Engineering\"\n-> CandidateTitle: \"Associate – Performance Engineering\"\n\nResume says:\n\"Fresh graduate with no professional experience\"\n-> CandidateTitle: \"FRESHER\"\n\nIf no current/recent title is available:\n-> CandidateTitle: null\n\n---------------------------------------\nSENIORITY LEVEL RULES\n---------------------------------------\n\nSeniorityLevel is mandatory and must always be returned.\n\nUse explicit total years of professional experience from attachment context first.\n\nDo NOT count years from:\n- phone numbers\n- dates\n- graduation years\n- certification years\n- project dates\n- unrelated numbers\n- percentages\n- cost savings\n- performance improvements\n- concurrent user counts\n\nMapping:\n- 0 years, fresher, no work experience, fresh graduate, or recent graduate with no professional experience -> FRESHER\n- 1 to 3 years -> JUNIOR\n- 4 years to less than 10 years -> INTERMEDIATE\n- 10 or more years, 10+ years, or over 10 years -> SENIOR\n\nIf years of experience are a range, use the lower bound unless the range starts at 10 or more.\n\nExamples:\n- 3-5 years -> JUNIOR\n- 4-6 years -> INTERMEDIATE\n- 10-12 years -> SENIOR\n\nIf explicit years are unavailable, use explicit seniority/title evidence only:\n- fresher, fresh graduate, no work experience -> FRESHER\n- junior, trainee, associate, entry level -> JUNIOR\n- mid-level, intermediate -> INTERMEDIATE\n- senior, lead, principal, staff, architect, manager, director, head -> SENIOR\n\nIf no explicit years and no explicit seniority/title evidence are available:\n- Return \"INTERMEDIATE\" as a mandatory fallback only.\n- Do not use this fallback as evidence for JobSearchKeywords, Title, CandidateTitle, or Description.\n\nExamples:\n\n\"Performance Test Engineer with 5+ years of experience\"\n-> SeniorityLevel: \"INTERMEDIATE\"\n\n\"12 years of experience\"\n-> SeniorityLevel: \"SENIOR\"\n\n\"2 years of experience\"\n-> SeniorityLevel: \"JUNIOR\"\n\n\"Fresh graduate\"\n-> SeniorityLevel: \"FRESHER\"\n\n---------------------------------------\nJOB SEARCH KEYWORDS RULES\n---------------------------------------\n\nIf SpecificJobNumber is not empty, follow the Critical Job ID Rule and skip this section.\n\nJobSearchKeywords must be:\n- A comma-separated string\n- NOT an array\n- Based only on explicit input values, except the allowed CandidateLocation normalizations above\n- Deduplicated case-insensitively while preserving first occurrence\n\nExample:\nJava, java, JAVA\n-> Java\n\nDo NOT include:\n- Years of experience\n- SeniorityLevel fallback values\n- Generic referral/search words:\n  refer, referral, candidate, resume, profile, job, jobs, role, roles, opening, openings, position, positions, please, opportunity, opportunities\n\nDo NOT include:\n- Excluded locations\n- Excluded roles\n- Excluded functions\n- Excluded industries\n- Excluded seniority\n- Excluded job types\n\nDo NOT create unsupported synonyms or expansions.\n\nExamples:\n- Java -> do NOT add J2EE\n- Spring -> do NOT add Spring Boot\n- AWS -> do NOT add Cloud\n- QA -> do NOT add Testing\n- Performance Testing -> do NOT add QA unless QA is explicitly present\n- Selenium -> do NOT add Automation unless automation is explicitly present\n\nOnly use values explicitly present in the input, except the allowed CandidateLocation normalizations above.\n\n---------------------------------------\nEXPLICIT REQUEST HANDLING\n---------------------------------------\n\nAfter the Critical Job ID Rule, employee request intent is the strongest signal.\n\nIf email subject or body explicitly requests:\n- location\n- role\n- title\n- function\n- category\n- seniority\n- industry\n- domain\n\nThen prioritize those requested values.\n\nIf requested CandidateLocation exists:\n- Put CandidateLocation first in JobSearchKeywords.\n- Do NOT add resume location.\n\nThen include:\n- Requested role/title/function/category/seniority/industry/domain\n- Resume-supported keywords that are directly relevant\n\nRemove generic words such as:\njobs, roles, openings, positions, opportunities\n\nDo NOT include unrelated resume keywords.\n\nExamples:\n\nEmail:\n\"Refer attached Candidate to USA Jobs\"\nResume:\nPerformance Test Engineer with JMeter, LoadRunner, AWS, Dynatrace, Datadog\n\nCandidateLocation:\n\"United States\"\n\nJobSearchKeywords may include:\nUnited States, Associate – Performance Engineering, Performance Engineering, Performance Testing, JMeter, LoadRunner, AWS, Dynatrace, Datadog\n\nDo NOT include:\nKochi, India\nbecause employee-requested CandidateLocation has priority.\n\n---------------------------------------\nGENERAL RESUME MATCHING\n---------------------------------------\n\nIf there is no specific job identifier and no stronger employee request, generate keywords from strongest explicit resume signals:\n\n1. CandidateLocation\n2. Requested role/title/function/category/seniority/industry/domain\n3. Current/recent title\n4. Explicit seniority\n5. Functional area\n6. Primary skills\n7. Tools/platforms/technologies\n8. Certifications\n9. Industry/domain\n\nInclude seniority only when explicitly present in the email or resume.\nDo NOT include SeniorityLevel if it was produced only by mandatory fallback.\nDo NOT derive seniority from years of experience alone for JobSearchKeywords.\n\nExamples:\n\"Senior Analyst\" -> include Senior Analyst\n\"10 years of experience as Analyst\" -> do NOT convert to Senior Analyst in JobSearchKeywords\n\n---------------------------------------\nSTRICT KEYWORD ORDER\n---------------------------------------\n\nReturn JobSearchKeywords in this exact order:\n\n1. SpecificJobNumber, only when present as the sole keyword value\n2. Employee-requested CandidateLocation\n3. Candidate current/residing/work CandidateLocation, only if no employee-requested CandidateLocation exists\n4. Requested role/title/function/category/seniority/industry/domain\n5. Current/recent title\n6. Explicit seniority\n7. Functional area\n8. Primary skills\n9. Tools/platforms/technologies\n10. Certifications\n11. Industry/domain\n\nPreserve first occurrence within each category.\nRemove duplicates case-insensitively.\n\n---------------------------------------\nKEYWORD QUALITY RULES\n---------------------------------------\n\nPrefer:\n- Specific job/requisition number when explicitly requested\n- Employee-requested CandidateLocation or role\n- Candidate current/residing/work CandidateLocation\n- Current title\n- Explicit seniority\n- Functional area\n- Skills\n- Technologies\n- Tools\n- Platforms\n- Certifications\n- Domain knowledge\n- Industry\n\nAvoid:\n- Generic words\n- Weak soft skills\n- Full sentences\n- Unsupported synonyms\n- Unsupported expansions\n- Duplicate terms\n- Candidate name\n- Email address\n- Phone number\n- Employer name unless it is also explicitly a domain/industry requirement\n\nDo not use weak words as standalone keywords:\nexperienced, responsible, worked, project, team, good, excellent, professional, motivated, dynamic, strong, proven, skilled\n\n---------------------------------------\nFIELD-SPECIFIC FINAL VALIDATION\n---------------------------------------\n\nBefore returning, validate every field:\n\nTitle:\n- Must be current/recent title only.\n- Must not be candidate name.\n- Must not come from fileName.\n- If not validated, return \"\".\n\nFileName:\n- Must be copied exactly from attachment fileName.\n- If missing, return \"\".\n\nDescription:\n- Must be supported by attachment context.\n- Must not include unsupported facts.\n- Must not include referral instructions.\n- If not validated, return \"\".\n\nJobSearchKeywords:\n- Must follow Critical Job ID Rule when SpecificJobNumber exists.\n- Must follow exclusion rules.\n- Must follow ordering rules.\n- Must not include generic referral/search words.\n- Must not contain unsupported synonyms or expansions.\n- Must be a comma-separated string.\n\nSpecificJobNumber:\n- Must come only from email subject or email body/content.\n- Must be empty if no specific job identifier is explicitly requested.\n\nCandidateLocation:\n- Must be employee-requested target job location or candidate current/present/residing/based/work location.\n- Must not be an excluded location.\n- Must not come from invalid sources.\n- Must not be derived from returned jobs or OM search results.\n- Must use CandidateLocation key, not Location key.\n\nCandidateTitle:\n- Must come only from attachment context.\n- Must be \"FRESHER\", an explicit actual current/recent title, or null.\n\nSeniorityLevel:\n- Must be exactly one of FRESHER, JUNIOR, INTERMEDIATE, SENIOR.\n\nNo value may come from ignored contentBase64, encoded content, binary content, OM search results, returned jobs, or external knowledge.\n\nIf a non-mandatory field cannot be validated, return \"\" for that field, except CandidateTitle which must be null when unavailable.\n\nReturn exactly one valid JSON object and nothing else."
            }
          ],
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Prepare Payload for Attachment Data",
            "returnType": "object",
            "sourceCode": "const attachment = $context.$nodes.GET_CANDIDATE_ATTACHMENT_INFORMATION.$output || {};\nconst input = $context.$triggers.REST.$input.candidateAttachment || {};\nconst firstAttachment =\n  Array.isArray(input.attachment) && input.attachment.length > 0\n    ? input.attachment[0] || {}\n    : {};\n\nfunction clean(value) {\n  if (value === null || value === undefined) {\n    return \"\";\n  }\n\n  const text = String(value).trim();\n\n  if (\n    text === \"\" ||\n    text.toLowerCase() === \"null\" ||\n    text.toLowerCase() === \"undefined\"\n  ) {\n    return \"\";\n  }\n\n  return text;\n}\n\nconst result = {\n  DatatypeCode: \"FILE\",\n  Title: clean(attachment.Title) || clean(attachment.CandidateTitle) || \"Candidate Resume\",\n  FileName: clean(attachment.FileName) || clean(firstAttachment.fileName) || \"Resume\",\n  \n  // Use whichever schema name you finalize.\n  JobSearchQuery: clean(attachment.JobSearchKeywords),\n  \n  SpecificJobNumber: clean(attachment.SpecificJobNumber),\n  CandidateLocation: clean(attachment.CandidateLocation),\n  CandidateTitle:\n    attachment.CandidateTitle === null ? null : clean(attachment.CandidateTitle),\n  SeniorityLevel: clean(attachment.SeniorityLevel).toUpperCase(),\n\n  FileContents: firstAttachment.contentBase64 || \"\"\n};\n\nreturn result;"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1781182086681"
          },
