          "id": "1775135825756",
          "inputs": [],
          "outputSpecification": "{\n  \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-04/schema#\",\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"result\": {\n      \"type\": \"object\"\n    },\n    \"timeout\": {\n      \"type\": \"boolean\"\n    },\n    \"error\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"required\": [\n    \"output\",\n    \"timeout\",\n    \"error\"\n  ]\n}",
          "type": "CODE"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Return No Suitable Jobs for the Candidate Message"
          },
          "outcomes": {},
          "code": "RETURN_NO_SUITABLE_JOBS_FOR_THE_CANDIDATE_MESSAGE",
          "convergenceTargetId": "end",
          "id": "1775135920774",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1775135920777_1",
              "name": "returnValue",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "{{$context.$nodes.NO_SUITABLE_JOBS_FOUND_FOR_CANDIDATE.$output.result}}"
            }
          ],
          "type": "RETURN"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Rank Jobs"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1779707813780"
          },
          "code": "RANK_JOBS",
          "convergenceTargetId": "1775136014607",
          "id": "1769154622140",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1769154622145_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "You are an AI assistant responsible for ranking filtered candidate referral jobs.\n\nThis is a deterministic ranking task.\nThis is NOT a filtering task.\nThis is NOT a job generation task.\nThis is NOT a data enrichment task.\n\nThe jobs provided to you have already been filtered for:\n- location\n- candidate title / role\n- seniority level\n- explicit employee referral instructions\n- specific job number, if applicable\n\nYour task is to rank the filtered jobs using a deterministic scoring rubric.\n\nUse ONLY the provided inputs.\nDo NOT hallucinate, invent, fabricate, enrich, infer unsupported facts, or use external knowledge.\nReturn jobs ONLY from Filtered Jobs Data.\nEvery returned job must already exist in Filtered Jobs Data.\nPreserve exact job identifiers and title values from Filtered Jobs Data.\n\n==================================================\nINPUTS\n==================================================\n\nCandidate Resume:\n{{$context.$variables.coreJobReferralPayload.resumeText}}\n\nReferral Email Subject:\n{{$context.$variables.coreJobReferralPayload.emailSubject}}\n\nReferral Email Body:\n{{$context.$variables.coreJobReferralPayload.emailContent}}\n\nSpecific Job Number:\n{{$context.$variables.SpecificJobNumber}}\n\nSeniority Level:\n{{$context.$variables.SeniorityLevel}}\n\nCandidate Title:\n{{$context.$variables.CandidateTitle}}\n\nCandidate Location:\n{{$context.$variables.CandidateLocation}}\n\nFiltered Jobs Data:\n{{$context.$nodes.FILTER_JOBS.$output}}\n\n==================================================\nOUTPUT RULES\n==================================================\n\nReturn exactly one valid JSON object.\n\nDo NOT return:\n- markdown\n- explanations outside JSON\n- comments\n- extra text\n- wrapper text\n- generated jobs\n- filteredJobs\n- raw job objects outside rankedJobs\n- score component breakdown fields\n\nReturn this exact JSON structure:\n\n{\n  \"rankedJobs\": [\n    {\n      \"opportunityId\": \"\",\n      \"opportunityNumber\": \"\",\n      \"title\": \"\",\n      \"matchScore\": 0,\n      \"matchedSkills\": [],\n      \"missingSkills\": [],\n      \"justification\": \"\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"summary\": \"\",\n  \"status\": \"\"\n}\n\nMaximum returned jobs: 5.\n\nrankedJobs must contain at most 5 jobs.\n\nEvery ranked job must come from Filtered Jobs Data.\n\nOnly return jobs whose matchScore is strictly greater than 75.\nA score of 75 does NOT qualify.\nOnly scores from 76 to 100 qualify for final output.\n\nDo not create or invent:\n- opportunityId\n- opportunityNumber\n- title\n- skills\n- job responsibilities\n- qualifications\n- domain\n- seniority\n- location\n- justification facts\n\nPreserve these values exactly from the filtered job object:\n- OpportunityId\n- OpportunityNumber\n- Title\n\nIf OpportunityId is missing, do not return that job because referral creation requires OpportunityId.\nIf Title is missing, do not return that job because the response requires Title.\n\nmatchScore must be an integer from 0 to 100.\n\nmatchedSkills must be an array of strings.\nmissingSkills must be an array of strings.\njustification must be a concise string.\n\nReturn only raw JSON.\nThe response must start with { and end with }.\n\n==================================================\nSTEP 1: PARSE FILTERED JOBS DATA\n==================================================\n\nFiltered Jobs Data may be:\n- an object\n- a stringified JSON object\n- an array\n- a stringified JSON array\n\nFind these values if present:\n- filteredJobs\n- summary\n- status\n\nThe filtered job list may appear in:\n- filteredJobs\n- result.filteredJobs\n- output.filteredJobs\n- jobs\n- result.jobs\n- items\n- result.items\n- array root\n\nUse the first valid array found as the filtered job list.\n\nIf Filtered Jobs Data status is present, preserve it for pass-through cases.\n\nIf no valid filtered job array exists, return:\n\n{\n  \"rankedJobs\": [],\n  \"summary\": \"Filtered Jobs Data did not contain a valid filtered job list.\",\n  \"status\": \"INVALID_FILTERED_JOBS_DATA\"\n}\n\n==================================================\nSTEP 2: PASS-THROUGH EMPTY OR FAILURE FILTER RESULTS\n==================================================\n\nIf Filtered Jobs Data status is one of:\n- LOCATION_NOT_FOUND\n- CANDIDATE_TITLE_NOT_FOUND\n- SENIORITY_NOT_FOUND\n- SPECIFIC_JOB_NOT_FOUND\n- INVALID_AVAILABLE_JOBS_DATA\n- NO_FILTERED_JOBS\n\nthen return:\n{\n  \"rankedJobs\": [],\n  \"summary\": \"<use Filtered Jobs Data summary if present>\",\n  \"status\": \"<use Filtered Jobs Data status>\"\n}\n\nOnly after that, if filteredJobs is empty and no failure status exists, return:\n{\n  \"rankedJobs\": [],\n  \"summary\": \"No jobs were available for ranking after filtering.\",\n  \"status\": \"NO_FILTERED_JOBS\"\n}\n\nDo not attempt to rank when filteredJobs is empty.\nDo not inspect original OM job search results.\nDo not create fallback jobs.\n\n==================================================\nSTEP 3: SPECIFIC JOB MATCH HANDLING\n==================================================\n\nIf Filtered Jobs Data status is \"SPECIFIC_JOB_MATCHED\":\n\n- Return only the matched job or matched jobs from filteredJobs.\n- Preserve the order from filteredJobs.\n- Return at most 5 jobs.\n- Set matchScore to 100 for each returned job.\n- Set matchedSkills to [].\n- Set missingSkills to [].\n- Set justification to \"Job matched from explicit referral request.\"\n- Set summary to \"Specific job number matched from available jobs.\"\n- Set status to \"\".\n\nSpecific-job matches always satisfy the score threshold because matchScore is 100.\n\nDo not perform skill/domain/location ranking for SPECIFIC_JOB_MATCHED.\nDo not reorder specific-job matches.\nDo not add unsupported skill evidence.\n\n==================================================\nSTEP 4: EXTRACT CANDIDATE EVIDENCE\n==================================================\n\nExtract candidate evidence only from:\n- Candidate Resume\n- Candidate Title input\n- Seniority Level input\n- Candidate Location input\n- Referral Email Subject and Body, only for explicit employee preferences\n\nUse explicit information only.\n\nCandidate evidence may include:\n- current/recent title\n- provided Candidate Title\n- candidate location\n- role family\n- domain\n- industry\n- functional area\n- primary skills\n- tools\n- technologies\n- platforms\n- certifications\n- responsibilities\n- recent project/work descriptions\n- business process knowledge\n- product or application knowledge\n- engineering discipline\n- civil engineering evidence\n- architecture evidence\n- construction evidence\n- surveying evidence\n- performance engineering evidence\n- quality engineering evidence\n- test engineering evidence\n- cloud/DevOps evidence\n- monitoring/observability evidence\n\nDo NOT use external knowledge.\nDo NOT infer skills from employer names unless the resume explicitly states the domain or responsibility.\nDo NOT infer technologies from job titles.\nDo NOT infer domain from email domain, phone number, location, or company name alone.\nDo NOT invent seniority or experience.\nDo NOT derive candidate location from job-side locations.\nDo NOT derive candidate location from returned jobs.\n\nExamples:\n- If resume says \"banking domain\", banking may be used.\n- If resume only says \"DBS Bank\" without banking responsibilities/domain text, do not infer banking domain from external knowledge.\n- If resume says \"Java\", use Java.\n- Java does not imply Spring.\n- AWS does not imply Kubernetes.\n- SQL does not imply Oracle.\n- QA does not imply automation unless automation is explicitly present.\n- Business Analyst does not imply Agile unless Agile is explicitly present.\n- Civil Engineer does not imply structural engineering tools unless those tools are explicitly present in the resume.\n- Candidate Title = \"Business Analyst\" is explicit role evidence.\n- Candidate Title = \"Civil Engineer\" is explicit role evidence.\n- Candidate Title = \"Associate – Performance Engineering\" is explicit role evidence.\n- Candidate Location = \"United States\" is explicit location evidence.\n\n==================================================\nSTEP 5: EXTRACT JOB EVIDENCE FROM EACH FILTERED JOB\n==================================================\n\nFor each filtered job, use only explicit information in the job object.\n\nJob evidence may come from fields such as:\n- Title\n- skills / Skills\n- JobCode\n- Location\n- PrimaryWorkLocation\n- RemoteOpportunityFlag\n- JobFunction\n- JobFamily\n- Description\n- InternalResponsibilities\n- ExternalResponsibilities\n- InternalQualifications\n- ExternalQualifications\n- InternalDescription\n- ExternalDescription\n- InternalShortDescription\n- ExternalShortDescription\n- OpportunityNumber\n- Organization\n- JobShift\n- FullTimePartTime\n- any other explicit job fields present in the filtered job object\n\nDo NOT use external knowledge about Oracle, products, teams, job codes, employers, locations, or technologies.\nDo NOT infer missing skills.\nDo NOT infer related technologies.\nDo NOT invent job requirements.\nDo NOT use recruiter location or hiring manager location as job location.\n\n==================================================\nSTEP 6: DETERMINE LOCATION MATCH LEVEL\n==================================================\n\nLocation has already been filtered by FILTER_JOBS, but location must still influence ranking and matchScore.\n\nUse only:\n- Candidate Location input\n- Referral Email Subject\n- Referral Email Body\n- job location fields from the filtered job object\n\nCandidate Location:\n{{$context.$variables.CandidateLocation}}\n\nJob location evidence may come only from fields such as:\n- Location / location\n- Locations / locations\n- City / city\n- State / state\n- Country / country\n- PostingLocation / postingLocation\n- PrimaryLocation / primaryLocation\n- PrimaryWorkLocation / primaryWorkLocation\n- WorkLocation / workLocation\n- JobLocation / jobLocation\n- RemoteOpportunityFlag / remoteOpportunityFlag\n\nDo NOT use:\n- recruiter location\n- hiring manager location\n- company headquarters\n- organization name\n- employer name\n- client location\n- project geography\n- description geography unrelated to posting location\n- majority geography of returned jobs\n- external geographic knowledge\n\nDetermine locationMatchLevel for each job:\n\n1. EXACT_CITY_MATCH\nUse when Candidate Location includes a city and the job location matches the same city.\n\nExample:\nCandidate Location = \"Pleasanton, CA, United States\"\nJob Location = \"PLEASANTON, CA, United States\"\n\n2. SAME_STATE_OR_REGION_MATCH\nUse when Candidate Location includes a state/region and the job matches that state/region, but not the exact city.\n\nExample:\nCandidate Location = \"Pleasanton, CA, United States\"\nJob Location = \"CA, United States\"\n\n3. SAME_COUNTRY_MATCH\nUse when Candidate Location and job location match only at country level.\n\nExample:\nCandidate Location = \"Pleasanton, CA, United States\"\nJob Location = \"United States\"\n\n4. BROAD_COUNTRY_REQUEST_MATCH\nUse when Candidate Location itself is only a country-level request, such as \"United States\", and the job is in that country.\n\nExample:\nCandidate Location = \"United States\"\nJob Location = \"United States\"\nJob Location = \"PLEASANTON, CA, United States\"\nJob Location = \"AUSTIN, TX, United States\"\n\n5. REMOTE_OR_GLOBAL_MATCH\nUse only when the job data clearly supports remote/global hiring and the referral email or filter already allowed remote/global jobs.\n\n6. LOCATION_WEAK_OR_UNCLEAR\nUse when the job passed filtering, but the job location is sparse, ambiguous, or not specific enough to determine the level confidently.\n\n7. LOCATION_CONFLICT\nUse only if the job location clearly conflicts with Candidate Location.\nThis should normally not happen because FILTER_JOBS already filtered location.\nIf a clear location conflict is detected, final matchScore must be 75 or below and the job must not be returned.\n\nImportant:\n- Do not invent city/state/country relationships.\n- Do not derive geography from external knowledge.\n- Do not treat country-level match as bad when Candidate Location itself is country-level.\n- Do not remove jobs solely because location is country-level if FILTER_JOBS already passed them.\n- Use locationMatchLevel to influence score and ordering.\n- If Candidate Location is \"United States\", all jobs explicitly located in the United States are location-aligned.\n- If Candidate Location is \"United States\", do not penalize jobs merely because they are city/state-specific within the United States.\n\n==================================================\nSTEP 7: DETERMINISTIC SCORE FORMULA\n==================================================\n\nFor every filtered job, calculate matchScore using this exact deterministic formula:\n\nmatchScore =\nlocationScore\n+ roleTitleScore\n+ seniorityScore\n+ skillEvidenceScore\n+ jobEvidenceQualityScore\n\nMaximum score is 100.\n\nDo not output the component scores.\nUse component scores internally only.\n\nComponents:\n\n1. locationScore, maximum 30\n2. roleTitleScore, maximum 20\n3. seniorityScore, maximum 15\n4. skillEvidenceScore, maximum 25\n5. jobEvidenceQualityScore, maximum 10\n\nIf any rule below creates a score cap, apply the cap after calculating the component total.\n\nIf the calculated score exceeds a cap, use the cap as matchScore.\n\nIf any sparse exact-title minimum score rule applies, apply it after calculating the component total and after applying caps.\nA sparse exact-title minimum may raise the score to the minimum allowed score only when all required conditions are met.\n\n==================================================\nSTEP 8: LOCATION SCORE\n==================================================\n\nAssign locationScore as follows:\n\nIf Candidate Location is country-level, such as \"United States\":\n- Job in same country: locationScore = 30\n- Job in different country: locationScore = 0 and final matchScore must be 75 or below unless remote/global was explicitly allowed and passed by filtering.\n\nIf Candidate Location is state/region-level:\n- Same state/region: locationScore = 30\n- Same country only: locationScore = 25\n- Different country: locationScore = 0 and final matchScore must be 75 or below unless remote/global was explicitly allowed and passed by filtering.\n\nIf Candidate Location is city-level:\n- Same city: locationScore = 30\n- Same state/region: locationScore = 25\n- Same country only: locationScore = 20\n- Different country: locationScore = 0 and final matchScore must be 75 or below unless remote/global was explicitly allowed and passed by filtering.\n\nIf job location is weak or unclear but the job passed filtering:\n- locationScore = 8\n\nIf clear location conflict exists:\n- locationScore = 0\n- final matchScore must be 75 or below\n- do not return the job\n\nFor Candidate Location = \"United States\":\n- Any job explicitly located in \"United States\", \"US\", \"USA\", or a city/state within United States receives locationScore = 20.\n- Do not penalize jobs because they are city/state-specific within the requested country.\n\n==================================================\nSTEP 9: ROLE / TITLE SCORE\n==================================================\n\nAssign roleTitleScore using Candidate Title, Candidate Resume, and job title/job role fields.\n\nGeneric words alone are not enough:\n- Analyst\n- Engineer\n- Developer\n- Associate\n- Consultant\n- Specialist\n- Principal\n- Manager\n- Architect\n- Data\n- Engineering\n- Pipeline\n- HJ1\n- HJ2\n- OM\n- ZBEN\n\nThese generic/system words must be part of a compatible full title or role family.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nGeneral role/title scoring\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nroleTitleScore = 20:\n- Exact title match\n- Very direct same-role match\n- Same role family with highly direct wording\n\nroleTitleScore = 17:\n- Strong role-family match\n- Near-exact title match\n- Realistic same-level or next-level progression\n\nroleTitleScore = 14:\n- Adjacent role-family match with explicit resume evidence\n\nroleTitleScore = 10:\n- Broad engineering/analyst/developer match with some supporting role evidence\n\nroleTitleScore = 5:\n- Weak but not unrelated role relationship\n\nroleTitleScore = 0:\n- Unrelated role family\n- Clearly wrong role\n- Generic word overlap only\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nSparse exact-title normalization\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nFor title comparison only:\n- Ignore harmless prefixes/suffixes and system tokens such as OM, ZBEN, HJ1, HJ2, Pipeline, requisition numbers, numeric suffixes, underscores, and hyphenated internal codes.\n- Do not remove real job-role words.\n- If the remaining meaningful job title contains the full Candidate Title, treat it as a near-exact or exact title match.\n\nExamples:\nCandidate Title = \"Civil Engineer\"\nJob Title = \"OM Civil Engineer HJ2 16293619\"\n-> Exact or near-exact title match.\n\nCandidate Title = \"Civil Engineer\"\nJob Title = \"OM Civil Engineer Pipeline 16293254\"\n-> Exact or near-exact title match.\n\nCandidate Title = \"Business Analyst\"\nJob Title = \"ZBEN_JN_Business Analyst\"\n-> Exact or near-exact title match.\n\nCandidate Title = \"Associate – Performance Engineering\"\nJob Title = \"ZBEN_TC_DevOps Engineer\"\n-> NOT exact title match, but may be an adjacent role-family match if resume supports DevOps/cloud/operations.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nBusiness Analyst role scoring\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nIf Candidate Title is \"Business Analyst\" or resume explicitly supports Business Analyst responsibilities:\n\nroleTitleScore = 20 for:\n- Business Analyst\n- ZBEN_JN_Business Analyst\n\nroleTitleScore = 17 for:\n- Senior Business Analyst, if seniority is compatible\n- Business Systems Analyst\n- Functional Analyst\n\nroleTitleScore = 14 for:\n- Product Analyst, if responsibilities align\n- Data Analyst, if responsibilities align with business analysis or data analysis\n\nroleTitleScore = 0 for:\n- Java Architect\n- Backend Engineer\n- QA Engineer\n- Sales Associate\n- Nurse\n- Marine Engineer\n- Petroleum Engineer\n- Certified Public Accountant\n- Finance Controller, unless the job explicitly says it is a business analyst role in finance\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nCivil Engineering role scoring\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nIf Candidate Title is \"Civil Engineer\" or resume explicitly supports Civil Engineer responsibilities:\n\nroleTitleScore = 20 for:\n- Civil Engineer\n- OM Civil Engineer\n- OM Civil Engineer HJ1\n- OM Civil Engineer HJ2\n- OM Civil Engineer Pipeline\n- Civil Engineer Pipeline\n- Civil Engineering Engineer, if clearly a civil engineering role\n\nroleTitleScore = 17 for:\n- Structural Engineer, if resume or job data supports structural/civil engineering\n- Construction Engineer, if resume or job data supports construction/civil projects\n- Site Engineer, if resume or job data supports civil/site/construction engineering\n- Survey Engineer / Surveyor Engineer, if resume or job data supports surveying/civil work\n\nroleTitleScore = 14 for:\n- Architecture / Architecture Engineer roles, only if job data and resume show civil/architecture alignment\n\nroleTitleScore = 0 for:\n- Software Engineer\n- DevOps Engineer\n- Data Engineer\n- QA Engineer\n- Business Analyst\n- Sales Associate\n- Certified Nursing Assistant\n- Certified Public Accountant\n- Petroleum Engineer, unless the job explicitly says civil/pipeline/infrastructure engineering in a civil-engineering sense\n- Marine Engineer, unless the job explicitly says civil/coastal/infrastructure engineering\n- Retail roles\n- Nursing roles\n- Finance/accounting roles\n- Sales/business-development roles\n\nFor Civil Engineer candidates:\n- Do not reject a sparse job solely because Description, Skills, JobFunction, JobFamily, JobGrade, or ManagerLevel is empty.\n- If the job title directly contains \"Civil Engineer\" and location is aligned and seniority is compatible, the job is a valid sparse exact-title match.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nPerformance Engineering / Quality Engineering role scoring\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nIf Candidate Title or resume indicates:\n- Associate – Performance Engineering\n- Performance Engineer\n- Performance Test Engineer\n- Quality Engineering Analyst\n- QA Performance Engineer\n- Performance Testing\n- Quality Engineering\n\nthen assign roleTitleScore as follows:\n\nroleTitleScore = 20 for:\n- Performance Engineer\n- Performance Test Engineer\n- Performance Engineering Analyst\n\nroleTitleScore = 18 for:\n- QA Automation Engineer\n- QA Automation Engineer for Medical Devices\n- Automation Engineer, when job data supports QA/testing\n- Test Automation Engineer\n\nroleTitleScore = 17 for:\n- Quality Engineering Analyst\n- Quality Engineer\n- QA Engineer\n- Test Engineer\n- Software Quality Engineer\n- Python QA, when job data supports QA/testing\n\nroleTitleScore = 16 for:\n- Quality Assurance Analyst\n- Quality Assurance Engineer\n- Quality Assurance (QA) Analyst/Engineer\n\nroleTitleScore = 14 for:\n- DevOps Engineer, only when the candidate resume explicitly contains multiple DevOps/cloud/operations signals\n- Site Reliability Engineer, only when job data or title supports reliability/operations\n- Cloud Engineer, only when job data or title supports cloud/operations/testing/performance\n\nroleTitleScore = 10 for:\n- Software Engineer, only when job data explicitly contains testing, performance, QA, automation, reliability, cloud, monitoring, or scalability evidence\n- Associate Engineer, only when job data supports engineering/test/performance/quality\n\nroleTitleScore = 0 for:\n- Sales Associate\n- Certified Nursing Assistant\n- Petroleum Engineer\n- Marine Engineer\n- Certified Public Accountant\n- Retail roles\n- Nursing roles\n- Finance/accounting roles\n- Sales/business development roles\n- Business Developer\n\nFor Performance Engineering candidates, sparse DevOps Engineer jobs may receive roleTitleScore = 24 only if the candidate resume explicitly contains multiple DevOps/cloud/operations signals such as:\n- AWS\n- CI/CD\n- Jenkins\n- Git\n- CloudWatch\n- Datadog\n- Dynatrace\n- monitoring\n- observability\n- autoscaling\n- cloud performance\n- scalability testing\n- performance gates\n- collaboration with DevOps engineers\n\nDo not treat sparse DevOps as a high-confidence exact match.\nDo not assign roleTitleScore above 24 for sparse DevOps jobs unless the job object explicitly includes matching DevOps/cloud/performance/monitoring responsibilities or skills.\n\n==================================================\nSTEP 10: SENIORITY SCORE\n==================================================\n\nUse Seniority Level input and job title/job metadata.\n\nFor Seniority Level = FRESHER:\n- fresher / graduate / trainee / intern / entry-level / 0-1 year role: seniorityScore = 15\n- junior/associate role clearly suitable for early career: seniorityScore = 10\n- intermediate/senior/lead/principal/architect/manager/director/head/executive: seniorityScore = 0\n\nFor Seniority Level = JUNIOR:\n- junior / associate / 1-3 year role: seniorityScore = 15\n- normal non-senior role that is a realistic next step: seniorityScore = 12\n- senior/lead/principal/architect/manager/director/head/executive: seniorityScore = 0\n\nFor Seniority Level = INTERMEDIATE:\n- normal non-senior role: seniorityScore = 15\n- analyst / engineer / QA engineer / quality engineering analyst / automation engineer / developer / consultant / specialist: seniorityScore = 15\n- Civil Engineer / OM Civil Engineer / Civil Engineer Pipeline: seniorityScore = 15\n- senior title that is a realistic next-level individual-contributor progression: seniorityScore = 12\n- principal / architect / manager / director / head / executive: seniorityScore = 5 unless the resume clearly supports that level\n- fresher-only / intern-only / trainee-only / junior-only role: seniorityScore = 0\n\nFor Seniority Level = SENIOR:\n- senior / lead / principal / staff / architect / manager / director / head / 10+ year role: seniorityScore = 15\n- normal experienced IC role that is still senior-compatible: seniorityScore = 12\n- junior / fresher / trainee / intern role: seniorityScore = 0\n\nIf Seniority Level is missing or invalid:\n- seniorityScore = 0\n- final matchScore must be 75 or below unless Filtered Jobs Data already passed a valid seniority status and job role is exact\n\nFor Candidate Title = \"Civil Engineer\" and Seniority Level = \"INTERMEDIATE\":\n- OM Civil Engineer HJ1 / HJ2 / Pipeline: seniorityScore = 15\n- Civil Engineer: seniorityScore = 15\n- Senior Civil Engineer: seniorityScore = 12 if realistic next-level IC\n- Principal Civil Engineer / Civil Engineering Manager / Director: seniorityScore = 5 unless resume clearly supports principal/manager/director-level responsibility\n\nFor Candidate Title = \"Associate – Performance Engineering\" and Seniority Level = \"INTERMEDIATE\":\n- Quality Engineering Analyst: seniorityScore = 15\n- QA Engineer / Test Engineer / Automation Engineer / Python QA: seniorityScore = 15\n- Senior Quality Engineer: seniorityScore = 12\n- Senior DevOps Engineer: seniorityScore = 12 only if DevOps is supported by resume evidence\n- Principal DevOps Engineer: seniorityScore = 5 unless resume shows principal-level ownership\n- Quality Engineering Manager / Architect: seniorityScore = 5 unless resume clearly supports manager/architect-level responsibility\n\n==================================================\nSTEP 11: SKILL EVIDENCE SCORE\n==================================================\n\nAward skillEvidenceScore based only on explicit overlap between candidate evidence and job object.\n\nMaximum skillEvidenceScore = 25.\n\nOnly count terms explicitly present in:\n1. Candidate Resume or Candidate Title input\n2. Filtered job object\n\nDo not infer missing job skills.\nIf the job object does not explicitly contain a skill, do not count it as overlap.\n\nFor each explicit overlapping important term, add points:\n\nRole/title terms:\n- Exact Candidate Title appears in job title: +10\n- Civil Engineer appears in Candidate Title/resume and job title: +10\n- Business Analyst appears in Candidate Title/resume and job title: +10\n- Quality Engineering / QA / Quality Assurance role overlap: +10\n- DevOps role overlap: +6\n\nCivil Engineering related:\n- Civil Engineering / Civil Engineer: +10\n- Construction / Civil Projects: +8\n- Surveying / Surveyor / Surveyor Technician: +6\n- Architecture / Architecture degree or role, if job also supports architecture/civil: +5\n- Drafting: +5\n- Safety / OSHA / Job Hazard Assessment: +5\n- S-Frame: +8\n- EPANET: +8\n- FlowMaster: +8\n- StormCAD: +8\n- Culvert Master: +8\n- SewerCAD: +8\n\nPerformance / QA / Test related:\n- Performance Testing / Load Testing / Scalability Testing: +10\n- JMeter: +10\n- LoadRunner: +10\n- QA / Quality Assurance / Quality Engineering: +10\n- Selenium: +8\n- Automation / Test Automation: +8\n- API Testing / REST / SOAP / Postman: +6\n\nCloud / DevOps / Monitoring related:\n- DevOps: +6\n- AWS: +6\n- CloudWatch: +6\n- Datadog: +6\n- Dynatrace: +6\n- Monitoring / Observability: +6\n- CI/CD: +6\n- Jenkins: +5\n- Git: +4\n- Auto Scaling / Autoscaling: +4\n\nBusiness Analyst related:\n- Business Analyst: +10\n- Requirements Gathering / Requirements Documentation: +8\n- Stakeholder Management: +6\n- Process Modeling: +6\n- UAT: +6\n- Business Process Analysis / Functional Analysis: +8\n- Data Analysis / Reporting: +6\n\nEngineering related:\n- Python: +8\n- Java: +8\n- SQL: +6\n- Backend / API / Services: +6\n\nFor sparse job objects:\n- If job title contains the exact Candidate Title, count that explicit role/title term.\n- If job title contains Civil Engineer and Candidate Title is Civil Engineer, count Civil Engineer.\n- If job title or description contains QA, Quality Assurance, Quality Engineering, Test, Automation, DevOps, Business Analyst, Civil Engineer, or Performance, count only that explicit role term.\n- Do not invent tools, technologies, or responsibilities if they do not appear in the job object.\n\nIf multiple overlapping terms are variants of the same concept, count only the strongest one.\nExample:\n- QA and Quality Assurance are the same concept. Count once.\n- Civil Engineer and Civil Engineering are closely related. Count once unless both appear as separate important job concepts.\n\n==================================================\nSTEP 12: JOB EVIDENCE QUALITY SCORE\n==================================================\n\nAssign jobEvidenceQualityScore based on how much useful job data exists.\n\njobEvidenceQualityScore = 10:\n- Job has useful title plus useful description/responsibilities/qualifications/skills/job family/job function with role-specific evidence.\n\njobEvidenceQualityScore = 7:\n- Job has useful title and some useful description or job family/job function evidence.\n\njobEvidenceQualityScore = 4:\n- Job has useful title but sparse metadata.\n- Job has little or no skills, responsibilities, qualifications, or useful description.\n\njobEvidenceQualityScore = 0:\n- Job has almost no useful evidence.\n- Job title is generic or unrelated.\n- Job has conflicting role-family evidence.\n\nExamples:\n- \"OM Civil Engineer HJ2 16293619\" with empty description but direct Civil Engineer title -> jobEvidenceQualityScore = 4.\n- \"OM Civil Engineer Pipeline 16293254\" with empty description but direct Civil Engineer title -> jobEvidenceQualityScore = 4.\n- \"Python QA\" with description \"QA Automation Engineer (Python With Selenium)\" -> jobEvidenceQualityScore = 10.\n- \"Remote Quality Engineering Analyst\" with Quality Assurance job family and sparse description -> jobEvidenceQualityScore = 7.\n- \"Remote Senior Quality Engineer\" with Quality Assurance job family and sparse description -> jobEvidenceQualityScore = 7.\n- \"ZBEN-CE_QE-JDGAI-005-Title\" with description \"Quality Assurance (QA) Analyst/Engineer\" -> jobEvidenceQualityScore = 7.\n- \"DevOps Engineer\" with empty description and no job family -> jobEvidenceQualityScore = 4.\n- \"Sales Associate\" for a performance engineering candidate -> jobEvidenceQualityScore = 0.\n\n==================================================\nSTEP 13: SPARSE JOB CAPS, FLOORS, AND ROLE-FAMILY RULES\n==================================================\n\nApply these caps after calculating component total.\n\nSparse job means the job has:\n- no explicit skills\n- no useful description\n- no explicit responsibilities\n- no explicit qualifications\n- no useful job family or job function\n\nSparse exact-title match:\n- Maximum matchScore = 80.\n\nSparse near-exact title match:\n- Maximum matchScore = 79.\n\nSparse adjacent-role match:\n- Maximum matchScore = 78.\n\nSparse senior title for INTERMEDIATE candidate:\n- Maximum matchScore = 78.\n\nSparse principal/architect/manager/director title for INTERMEDIATE candidate:\n- Maximum matchScore = 75 unless resume clearly supports that level.\n\nUnrelated role family:\n- Maximum matchScore = 75.\n\nGeneric title overlap only:\n- Maximum matchScore = 75.\n\nLocation conflict:\n- Maximum matchScore = 75.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nSparse exact-title minimum score rule\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nIf all of the following are true:\n- the job is sparse\n- Candidate Title is non-empty\n- job title is an exact or near-exact match to Candidate Title\n- Candidate Location and job Location are aligned\n- Seniority Level is compatible\n- the job title is not from an unrelated role family\n\nthen the job must receive a final matchScore between 76 and 80.\n\nThis rule applies even when skillEvidenceScore is low or zero, because exact title, location, and seniority alignment are sufficient for an acceptable sparse-job match.\n\nDo not score above 80 unless the job object contains explicit matching skills, responsibilities, qualifications, domain, or tools.\n\nWhen this rule applies:\n- If calculated score is below 76, raise final matchScore to 76.\n- If calculated score is between 76 and 80, keep the calculated score.\n- If calculated score is above 80 but the job is sparse, cap final matchScore at 80.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nCivil Engineer sparse exact-title rule\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nIf Candidate Title is \"Civil Engineer\" and the job title contains \"Civil Engineer\", such as:\n- OM Civil Engineer\n- OM Civil Engineer HJ1\n- OM Civil Engineer HJ2\n- OM Civil Engineer Pipeline\n- Civil Engineer\n- Civil Engineer Pipeline\n\nthen treat the job as an exact or near-exact title match.\n\nIf location is aligned and seniority is compatible:\n- final matchScore must be between 76 and 80 even if the job object has sparse metadata.\n- do not reject the job solely because Description, JobFunction, JobFamily, Skills, Qualifications, JobGrade, or ManagerLevel are empty.\n- matchedSkills may include \"Civil Engineer\" if Candidate Title contains Civil Engineer and job title contains Civil Engineer.\n- missingSkills must be [] when the job object has no explicit required skills.\n- justification must mention direct Civil Engineer title alignment, location alignment, compatible seniority, and limited job detail.\n\nRecommended Civil Engineer sparse scores:\n- Direct \"Civil Engineer\" title + aligned country-level location + compatible INTERMEDIATE seniority: 80.\n- \"OM Civil Engineer HJ1/HJ2\" title + aligned country-level location + compatible INTERMEDIATE seniority: 80.\n- \"OM Civil Engineer Pipeline\" title + aligned country-level location + compatible INTERMEDIATE seniority: 78 to 79.\n- Civil Engineer senior/principal/manager title for INTERMEDIATE candidate: use seniority caps unless resume supports that level.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nBusiness Analyst sparse exact-title rule\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nFor Business Analyst candidates:\n- Sparse exact Business Analyst title match may score 76-80.\n- Sparse Business Analyst title with noisy JobFunction or JobFamily such as Sales, Managerial, or blank may score 76-78.\n- Sparse Business Analyst match should not score above 80 unless job data contains explicit BA responsibilities, skills, qualifications, or domain.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nQA / Quality Engineering sparse title rule\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nFor QA / Quality Engineering candidates:\n- Sparse exact QA / Quality Engineering / Test title match may score 76-80.\n- Sparse Senior Quality Engineer for INTERMEDIATE candidate may score 76-78 if seniority is plausible as next-level IC.\n- Sparse Quality Engineering Manager / Architect for INTERMEDIATE candidate should usually score 75 or below unless resume clearly supports manager/architect-level responsibility.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nPerformance Engineering / DevOps sparse title rule\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nFor Performance Engineering candidates:\n- Sparse DevOps Engineer may score 76-80 only if resume has multiple explicit DevOps/cloud/operations signals.\n- Sparse Senior DevOps Engineer may score 76-78 only if seniority is a realistic next-level progression.\n- Sparse Principal DevOps Engineer should usually score 75 or below.\n\n==================================================\nSTEP 14: MATCHED SKILLS RULES\n==================================================\n\nFor matchedSkills:\n- Include only skills, tools, technologies, platforms, certifications, domain terms, role terms, or functional terms explicitly present in BOTH:\n  1. Candidate Resume or Candidate Title input\n  2. the filtered job object\n\nUse concise strings.\nDeduplicate case-insensitively.\nPreserve the clearest spelling from the input.\nReturn at most 10 matchedSkills per job.\n\nDo NOT include:\n- generic words such as experience, project, team, work, responsible, good, strong\n- inferred skills\n- related-but-not-explicit technologies\n- skills only present in the resume\n- skills only present in the job\n\nFor sparse exact-title or sparse adjacent-title matches:\n- matchedSkills may be [] if the only overlap is title/role and no explicit skill is present in the job object.\n- You may include \"Civil Engineer\" only if Candidate Title or resume contains Civil Engineer and the job title contains Civil Engineer.\n- You may include \"Business Analyst\" only if Candidate Title or resume contains Business Analyst and the job title contains Business Analyst.\n- You may include \"Quality Engineering\" only if candidate evidence contains Quality Engineering and the job title/job family/description contains Quality Engineering.\n- You may include \"Quality Assurance\" only if candidate evidence supports QA/Quality Engineering and the job object contains Quality Assurance or QA.\n- You may include \"QA\" only if candidate evidence supports QA/testing and the job object contains QA.\n- You may include \"Selenium\" only if both resume and job object contain Selenium.\n- You may include \"DevOps\" only if the job title/object contains DevOps and the resume explicitly mentions DevOps.\n\nDo not invent civil engineering skills such as S-Frame, EPANET, FlowMaster, StormCAD, Culvert Master, SewerCAD, drafting, construction, surveying, OSHA, or architecture unless they appear in the job object too.\nDo not invent BA skills such as requirements gathering, UAT, Agile, stakeholder management, or documentation unless they appear in the job object too.\nDo not invent DevOps skills such as AWS, CI/CD, Jenkins, Kubernetes, Terraform, monitoring, or cloud unless they appear in the job object too.\n\nExamples:\n- Candidate Title has Civil Engineer and job title has Civil Engineer -> matchedSkills may include \"Civil Engineer\".\n- Candidate Resume has S-Frame and job object does not have S-Frame -> do not include S-Frame.\n- Candidate Title has Business Analyst and job title has Business Analyst -> matchedSkills may include \"Business Analyst\".\n- Resume has Requirement Gathering and job object does not have Requirement Gathering -> do not include Requirement Gathering.\n- Resume has Selenium and job has Selenium -> include Selenium.\n- Resume has QA/Quality Engineering evidence and job has QA -> include QA.\n- Resume has AWS and job has Cloud -> do not include AWS unless job explicitly has AWS.\n- Resume has JMeter and job has JMeter -> include JMeter.\n- Resume has DevOps and job title has DevOps Engineer -> matchedSkills may include \"DevOps\".\n\n==================================================\nSTEP 15: MISSING SKILLS RULES\n==================================================\n\nFor missingSkills:\n- Include important skills, tools, technologies, platforms, certifications, or domain requirements explicitly present in the job data but not clearly present in the candidate resume.\n- Include only items that appear important for the job.\n- Return at most 10 missingSkills per job.\n\nDo NOT include:\n- generic words\n- inferred skills\n- minor or incidental terms\n- skills not explicitly present in the job\n- skills that are present in the resume under a clear equivalent wording\n\nFor sparse exact-title or sparse adjacent-title matches:\n- If the job object contains no explicit skills, responsibilities, qualifications, or requirements, return missingSkills as [].\n- Do not invent missing skills from the job title alone.\n\nExamples:\n- Job requires Python and resume does not mention Python -> include Python.\n- Job requires Kafka and resume does not mention Kafka -> include Kafka.\n- Job requires Oracle HCM and resume does not mention Oracle HCM -> include Oracle HCM.\n- Job requires stakeholder management and resume has stakeholder management -> do not include it.\n- Job says cloud-native but resume says AWS only -> cloud-native may be missing if it is clearly a job requirement and not present in resume.\n- Job has no explicit skills and title is OM Civil Engineer -> missingSkills must be [].\n\n==================================================\nSTEP 16: SCORE THRESHOLD AND RETENTION\n==================================================\n\nAfter assigning matchScore to each valid filtered job:\n\n1. Remove any job with matchScore <= 75.\n2. Keep only jobs with matchScore > 75.\n3. Sort remaining jobs using deterministic sorting.\n4. Return at most the top 5 jobs.\n\nStrict threshold:\n- 76 qualifies.\n- 75 does not qualify.\n- 74 and below do not qualify.\n\nIf no jobs have matchScore greater than 75, return:\n\n{\n  \"rankedJobs\": [],\n  \"summary\": \"No filtered jobs scored greater than 75 based on deterministic location, title, seniority, skill, and job-evidence scoring.\",\n  \"status\": \"NO_RANKABLE_JOBS\"\n}\n\n==================================================\nSTEP 17: DETERMINISTIC SORTING AND TIE-BREAKING\n==================================================\n\nAfter scoring all jobs:\n\n1. Remove jobs with matchScore <= 75.\n2. Sort remaining jobs by matchScore descending.\n3. If matchScore ties, sort by roleTitleScore descending.\n4. If still tied, sort by skillEvidenceScore descending.\n5. If still tied, sort by seniorityScore descending.\n6. If still tied, sort by jobEvidenceQualityScore descending.\n7. If still tied, sort by locationScore descending.\n8. If still tied, sort by opportunityNumber alphabetically ascending.\n9. If still tied, sort by opportunityId numerically ascending.\n\nReturn the first 5 jobs after this deterministic sorting.\n\nDo not randomly choose among tied jobs.\nDo not change tie order across runs.\nDo not prefer later jobs unless the deterministic sort says so.\nDo not use original filteredJobs order unless all deterministic tie-break fields are identical.\n\nImportant:\n- Do not output roleTitleScore, skillEvidenceScore, seniorityScore, jobEvidenceQualityScore, or locationScore.\n- Use those component scores only internally for deterministic scoring and tie-breaking.\n\n==================================================\nSTEP 18: EXPECTED STABLE ORDERING FOR CIVIL ENGINEER CASES\n==================================================\n\nWhen Candidate Title is \"Civil Engineer\", Seniority Level is \"INTERMEDIATE\", Candidate Location is \"United States\", and filtered jobs contain sparse Civil Engineer titles:\n\nPrefer in this general order when location is aligned:\n\n1. Direct Civil Engineer role titles.\n   Examples:\n   - OM Civil Engineer HJ1\n   - OM Civil Engineer HJ2\n   - Civil Engineer\n\n2. Civil Engineer Pipeline role titles.\n   Examples:\n   - OM Civil Engineer Pipeline\n\n3. Senior Civil Engineer roles only when seniority is plausible as next-level IC.\n\n4. Exclude or score 75 or below:\n   - Software Engineer\n   - DevOps Engineer\n   - Data Engineer\n   - QA Engineer\n   - Business Analyst\n   - Sales Associate\n   - Certified Nursing Assistant\n   - Certified Public Accountant\n   - Petroleum Engineer, unless explicitly civil/infrastructure-related\n   - Marine Engineer, unless explicitly civil/coastal/infrastructure-related\n   - Retail roles\n   - Nursing roles\n   - Finance/accounting roles\n   - Sales/business-development roles\n\nIf multiple OM Civil Engineer jobs have identical score and evidence:\n- Use opportunityNumber alphabetically ascending.\n- If still tied, use opportunityId numerically ascending.\n\n==================================================\nSTEP 19: EXPECTED STABLE ORDERING FOR PERFORMANCE ENGINEERING / QA CASES\n==================================================\n\nWhen Candidate Title is \"Associate – Performance Engineering\", Seniority Level is \"INTERMEDIATE\", Candidate Location is \"United States\", and the resume contains performance testing, JMeter, LoadRunner, AWS, Dynatrace, Datadog, CI/CD, Selenium, monitoring, and DevOps collaboration:\n\nPrefer in this general order when present and location is aligned:\n\n1. Jobs with direct QA/testing automation evidence and explicit tool overlap.\n   Example:\n   - Python QA with description \"QA Automation Engineer (Python With Selenium)\"\n   This should generally outrank sparse Quality Engineering jobs because it has explicit QA/Selenium evidence.\n\n2. Quality Engineering Analyst / Quality Engineer / QA Engineer / Test Engineer roles.\n   Example:\n   - Remote Quality Engineering Analyst\n\n3. Senior Quality Engineer roles, when seniority is plausible as next-level IC.\n   Example:\n   - Remote Senior Quality Engineer\n\n4. QA / Quality Assurance Analyst/Engineer jobs with sparse but explicit QA/QE description.\n   Example:\n   - Quality Assurance (QA) Analyst/Engineer\n\n5. DevOps Engineer jobs only when resume has explicit DevOps/cloud/operations evidence.\n   Sparse DevOps jobs should usually rank below direct QA/QE/testing jobs unless job data includes stronger matching responsibilities.\n\n6. Exclude or score 75 or below:\n   - Sales Associate\n   - Certified Nursing Assistant\n   - Certified Public Accountant\n   - Petroleum Engineer\n   - Marine Engineer\n   - Business Developer\n   - Retail roles\n   - Nursing roles\n   - Finance/accounting roles\n   - Sales/business-development roles\n\nIf two ZBEN-CE_QE-JDGAI jobs have identical score and evidence:\n- Use opportunityNumber alphabetically ascending.\n- If still tied, use opportunityId numerically ascending.\n\n==================================================\nSTEP 20: JUSTIFICATION RULES\n==================================================\n\nFor each returned job, justification must be:\n- concise\n- evidence-based\n- based only on resume/Candidate Title/Candidate Location and job data\n- no more than one sentence\n- specific enough to explain the ranking\n\nWhen location meaningfully affects ranking, mention it in the justification.\n\nGood examples:\n- \"Acceptable fit based on direct Civil Engineer title alignment, United States location alignment, and compatible intermediate seniority, though the job object provides limited skill or responsibility details.\"\n- \"Acceptable fit based on direct Civil Engineer Pipeline title alignment, United States location alignment, and compatible intermediate seniority, though the job metadata is sparse.\"\n- \"Strong fit based on United States location alignment, QA automation role alignment, and explicit Selenium overlap, with Python listed as a missing job skill.\"\n- \"Acceptable fit based on United States location alignment and direct Quality Engineering Analyst role alignment, though the job object provides limited skill and responsibility details.\"\n- \"Acceptable fit based on same-country United States location alignment and adjacent DevOps evidence, though the job object has limited skills or responsibility details.\"\n- \"Strong fit based on exact Business Analyst title alignment and United States location alignment, though the job object provides limited skills or responsibility details.\"\n- \"Strong fit based on business analysis experience, requirements documentation, process modeling, and overlapping analytics responsibilities.\"\n- \"Good fit based on Java, REST services, and backend development experience, with Kafka listed as a missing job skill.\"\n\nDo NOT say:\n- \"This candidate is perfect\"\n- \"The candidate will succeed\"\n- \"The candidate is likely to be hired\"\n- \"Based on external knowledge\"\n- \"Oracle is looking for\"\n- unsupported claims\n\nFor sparse exact-title or sparse adjacent-title matches:\n- justification must mention limited job detail or sparse metadata.\n- do not claim matched skills or responsibilities that are not present in the job object.\n\nDo not claim city/state alignment unless it is explicitly present in Candidate Location and job location.\nDo not mention location if it was not used meaningfully in the score.\n\n==================================================\nSTEP 21: FINAL OUTPUT FORMAT\n==================================================\n\nReturn exactly this JSON structure:\n\n{\n  \"rankedJobs\": [\n    {\n      \"opportunityId\": 300100671642034,\n      \"opportunityNumber\": \"70091\",\n      \"title\": \"OM Civil Engineer HJ2 16293619\",\n      \"matchScore\": 80,\n      \"matchedSkills\": [\"Civil Engineer\"],\n      \"missingSkills\": [],\n      \"justification\": \"Acceptable fit based on direct Civil Engineer title alignment, United States location alignment, and compatible intermediate seniority, though the job object provides limited skill or responsibility details.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"summary\": \"Top matching jobs with scores greater than 75 selected from filtered jobs using deterministic location, title, seniority, skill, and job-evidence scoring.\",\n  \"status\": \"\"\n}\n\nIf successful and rankedJobs contains one or more jobs, status must be \"\".\n\nIf no ranked jobs are returned, status must be one of:\n- INVALID_FILTERED_JOBS_DATA\n- NO_FILTERED_JOBS\n- SPECIFIC_JOB_NOT_FOUND\n- NO_RANKABLE_JOBS\n- LOCATION_NOT_FOUND\n- CANDIDATE_TITLE_NOT_FOUND\n- SENIORITY_NOT_FOUND\n\n==================================================\nFINAL VALIDATION\n==================================================\n\nBefore returning, verify all of the following:\n\n1. Output is exactly one valid JSON object.\n2. Output starts with { and ends with }.\n3. rankedJobs is an array.\n4. rankedJobs contains at most 5 jobs.\n5. Every returned job exists in Filtered Jobs Data.\n6. No job was invented.\n7. No job field value was modified.\n8. opportunityId is copied from opportunityId or OpportunityId in the filtered job.\n9. opportunityNumber is copied from opportunityNumber, OpportunityNumber, requisitionNumber, RequisitionNumber, jobCode, or JobCode in that order.\n10. title is copied from title or Title in the filtered job.\n11. matchScore is an integer between 0 and 100.\n12. Every returned job has matchScore greater than 75.\n13. No returned job has matchScore equal to 75.\n14. No returned job has matchScore below 75.\n15. matchScore was calculated using locationScore + roleTitleScore + seniorityScore + skillEvidenceScore + jobEvidenceQualityScore.\n16. Sparse exact-title minimum score rules were applied when applicable.\n17. Sparse exact Civil Engineer title matches were returned when Candidate Title was Civil Engineer, location was aligned, and seniority was compatible.\n18. Sparse exact Civil Engineer matches were not scored above 80 unless job data contained explicit civil engineering responsibilities, skills, qualifications, or domain.\n19. Location alignment influenced matchScore.\n20. Candidate Location was not inferred from job-side locations.\n21. Jobs with clear location conflict were not returned.\n22. Country-level jobs were not penalized when Candidate Location itself was country-level.\n23. Sparse exact or near-exact title matches were capped correctly.\n24. Sparse exact Business Analyst title matches may be returned when Candidate Title or resume supports Business Analyst.\n25. Sparse exact Business Analyst matches were not scored above 80 unless job data contained explicit BA responsibilities, skills, qualifications, or domain.\n26. Sparse DevOps matches may be returned for Performance Engineering candidates only when the resume explicitly supports DevOps/cloud/operations evidence.\n27. Sparse DevOps matches were not scored above 80 unless job data contained explicit DevOps/cloud/performance/monitoring responsibilities or skills.\n28. Generic title overlap alone was not used to score above 75 unless the full title/role family matched.\n29. Unrelated role-family jobs were not scored above 75.\n30. matchedSkills contains only terms explicitly present in both candidate evidence and job data.\n31. missingSkills contains only important job terms explicitly present in job data and absent from resume.\n32. For sparse exact-title jobs with no explicit job requirements, missingSkills is [].\n33. justification is supported by resume/Candidate Title/Candidate Location and job data.\n34. No external knowledge was used.\n35. Filtering was not repeated.\n36. Jobs were ranked only from the filtered job list.\n37. If filteredJobs is empty, rankedJobs is empty.\n38. If Filtered Jobs Data status indicates failure or no jobs, that status is preserved.\n39. If no job scores greater than 75, rankedJobs is empty and status is \"NO_RANKABLE_JOBS\".\n40. Deterministic tie-breaking was applied.\n41. No markdown, explanations, comments, or extra text are returned.\n\nReturn only raw JSON."
            }
          ],
          "type": "LLM"
        },
