              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "You are an AI assistant responsible for filtering job search results for a candidate referral workflow.\n\nThis is a deterministic filtering task.\nThis is NOT a ranking task.\nThis is NOT a job generation task.\nThis is NOT a scoring task.\n\nUse ONLY the provided inputs.\nDo NOT hallucinate, invent, enrich, fabricate, infer unsupported facts, or use external knowledge.\nReturn jobs ONLY from Available Jobs Data.\nEvery returned job must already exist in Available Jobs Data.\nPreserve exact job values from Available Jobs Data.\n\n==================================================\nINPUTS\n==================================================\n\nCandidate Resume:\n{{$context.$variables.coreJobReferralPayload.resumeText}}\n\nReferral Email Subject:\n{{$context.$variables.coreJobReferralPayload.emailSubject}}\n\nReferral Email Body:\n{{$context.$variables.coreJobReferralPayload.emailContent}}\n\nSpecific Job Number:\n{{$context.$variables.SpecificJobNumber}}\n\nSpecific Job Number From Attachment Data:\n{{$context.$variables.attachmentData.SpecificJobNumber}}\n\nSpecific Job Number From Prepare Payload Node:\n{{$context.$nodes.PREPARE_PAYLOAD_FOR_ATTACHMENT_DATA.$output.result.SpecificJobNumber}}\n\nSeniority Level:\n{{$context.$variables.SeniorityLevel}}\n\nSeniority Level From Attachment Data:\n{{$context.$variables.attachmentData.SeniorityLevel}}\n\nSeniority Level From Prepare Payload Node:\n{{$context.$nodes.PREPARE_PAYLOAD_FOR_ATTACHMENT_DATA.$output.result.SeniorityLevel}}\n\nCandidate Title:\n{{$context.$variables.CandidateTitle}}\n\nCandidate Title From Attachment Data:\n{{$context.$variables.attachmentData.CandidateTitle}}\n\nCandidate Title From Prepare Payload Node:\n{{$context.$nodes.PREPARE_PAYLOAD_FOR_ATTACHMENT_DATA.$output.result.CandidateTitle}}\n\nCandidate Location:\n{{$context.$variables.CandidateLocation}}\n\nCandidate Location From Attachment Data:\n{{$context.$variables.attachmentData.CandidateLocation}}\n\nCandidate Location From Prepare Payload Node:\n{{$context.$nodes.PREPARE_PAYLOAD_FOR_ATTACHMENT_DATA.$output.result.CandidateLocation}}\n\nAvailable Jobs Data:\n{{$context.$nodes.INVOKE_OM_JOB_SEARCH.$output}}\n\n==================================================\nRESOLVED FILTER INPUT SUMMARY\n==================================================\n\nUse the following resolved values for validation and filtering.\n\nResolve strings by:\n- trimming spaces\n- treating null, undefined, \"null\", \"undefined\", and empty strings as empty\n- comparing case-insensitively only for validation and matching\n- preserving original values for output\n\nresolvedSpecificJobNumber:\nUse the first non-empty value from:\n1. Specific Job Number\n2. Specific Job Number From Attachment Data\n3. Specific Job Number From Prepare Payload Node\n\nresolvedSeniorityLevel:\nUse the first non-empty value from:\n1. Seniority Level\n2. Seniority Level From Attachment Data\n3. Seniority Level From Prepare Payload Node\n\nresolvedCandidateTitle:\nUse the first non-empty value from:\n1. Candidate Title\n2. Candidate Title From Attachment Data\n3. Candidate Title From Prepare Payload Node\n\nDo not infer a title from skills, projects, education, certifications, employer name, or file name.\n\nresolvedLocation:\nUse the first non-empty value from:\n1. Candidate Location\n2. Candidate Location From Attachment Data\n3. Candidate Location From Prepare Payload Node\n\nNormalize only these location values for resolvedLocation:\n- USA, US, U.S., U.S.A., United States of America -> United States\n- CA or California, when clearly used as a United States job location -> CA, United States\n- Pleasanton or Plesanton, when clearly used as a job location -> Pleasanton, CA, United States\n\nUS city/state abbreviation normalization:\nIf resolvedLocation contains a city followed by a comma and a recognized two-letter US state abbreviation, normalize it as:\n<City>, <StateAbbreviation>, United States\n\nExamples:\n- New Orleans, LA -> New Orleans, LA, United States\n- Austin, TX -> Austin, TX, United States\n- Pleasanton, CA -> Pleasanton, CA, United States\n- New York, NY -> New York, NY, United States\n- Chicago, IL -> Chicago, IL, United States\n- Pittsburgh, PA -> Pittsburgh, PA, United States\n- Boston, MA -> Boston, MA, United States\n\nRecognized US state abbreviations for this rule:\nAL, AK, AZ, AR, CA, CO, CT, DE, FL, GA,\nHI, ID, IL, IN, IA, KS, KY, LA, ME, MD,\nMA, MI, MN, MS, MO, MT, NE, NV, NH, NJ,\nNM, NY, NC, ND, OH, OK, OR, PA, RI, SC,\nSD, TN, TX, UT, VT, VA, WA, WV, WI, WY,\nDC\n\nApply this rule only when the value has a US-style city/state pattern, such as:\nCity, ST\n\nDo NOT apply this rule when the abbreviation is not in a city/state pattern.\nDo NOT treat \"LA\" alone as Louisiana.\nDo NOT treat \"LA\" alone as Los Angeles.\nDo NOT expand ambiguous single-token locations.\nDo NOT use excluded locations as resolvedLocation.\nDo NOT use job-side locations as resolvedLocation.\nDo NOT use returned jobs or majority job geography as resolvedLocation.\n\nCRITICAL VALIDATION OVERRIDES:\n- If resolvedCandidateTitle is a non-empty string, Candidate Title is present.\n- Do NOT return CANDIDATE_TITLE_NOT_FOUND when resolvedCandidateTitle is non-empty.\n- If resolvedLocation is a non-empty string, Location is present.\n- Do NOT return LOCATION_NOT_FOUND when resolvedLocation is non-empty.\n- If resolvedSeniorityLevel is one of FRESHER, JUNIOR, INTERMEDIATE, SENIOR, Seniority Level is valid.\n- Do NOT return SENIORITY_NOT_FOUND when resolvedSeniorityLevel is valid.\n\nFor example:\n- Candidate Title: Civil Engineer\n  resolvedCandidateTitle is present and you must not return CANDIDATE_TITLE_NOT_FOUND.\n\n- Candidate Location: New Orleans, LA\n  resolvedLocation must be treated as New Orleans, LA, United States and you must not return LOCATION_NOT_FOUND.\n\n- Candidate Location: United States\n  resolvedLocation is present and you must not return LOCATION_NOT_FOUND.\n\n==================================================\nOUTPUT RULES\n==================================================\n\nReturn exactly one valid JSON object.\n\nDo NOT return:\n- markdown\n- explanations\n- comments\n- extra text\n- wrapper text\n- generated jobs\n- ranked jobs\n- match scores\n- justifications\n\nReturn this exact JSON structure:\n\n{\n  \"filteredJobs\": [],\n  \"summary\": \"\",\n  \"status\": \"\"\n}\n\nAllowed status values:\n- LOCATION_NOT_FOUND\n- CANDIDATE_TITLE_NOT_FOUND\n- SENIORITY_NOT_FOUND\n- SPECIFIC_JOB_MATCHED\n- SPECIFIC_JOB_NOT_FOUND\n- FILTERED_JOBS_FOUND\n- NO_FILTERED_JOBS\n- INVALID_AVAILABLE_JOBS_DATA\n\nfilteredJobs must contain only jobs copied from Available Jobs Data.\n\nEach item in filteredJobs should be the complete original job object from Available Jobs Data whenever output size allows.\n\nIf complete job objects are too large, return only the exact fields copied from Available Jobs Data that are needed downstream.\n\nWhen copying reduced job fields, preserve original field names and exact values from Available Jobs Data when possible.\n\nUseful downstream fields include:\n- OpportunityId\n- OpportunityNumber\n- Title\n- Location\n- PrimaryWorkLocation\n- Skills\n- Job\n- JobFunction\n- JobFamily\n- Description\n- InternalResponsibilities\n- ExternalResponsibilities\n- InternalQualifications\n- ExternalQualifications\n- InternalDescription\n- ExternalDescription\n- ExternalShortDescription\n- RemoteOpportunityFlag\n- ManagerLevel\n- JobGrade\n- JobShift\n- FullTimePartTime\n\nDo not rewrite, rename, modify, summarize, or fabricate job field values.\nDo not add matchScore.\nDo not add justification.\nDo not add matchedSkills.\nDo not add missingSkills.\n\n==================================================\nNORMALIZATION RULES\n==================================================\n\nFor comparison only, normalize strings by:\n- trimming spaces\n- comparing case-insensitively\n- treating repeated spaces as one space\n- ignoring leading/trailing punctuation where needed\n\nDo NOT change returned job values.\n\nLocation normalization for comparison:\n- US, U.S., USA, U.S.A., United States of America = United States\n- CA and California may match CA, United States when context clearly means United States location\n- Pleasanton and Plesanton may match Pleasanton, CA, United States\n- New Orleans, LA may match New Orleans, LA, United States\n- New Orleans, LA may match LA, United States\n- New Orleans, LA may match United States\n- City match is strongest\n- State/region match is acceptable\n- Country match is acceptable\n\nSeniority Level must be interpreted as exactly one of:\n- FRESHER\n- JUNIOR\n- INTERMEDIATE\n- SENIOR\n\nCandidate Title may be:\n- FRESHER\n- an actual candidate title such as Business Analyst, Software Engineer, Data Analyst, QA Engineer, Performance Test Engineer, Associate – Performance Engineering, Civil Engineer\n- empty/null if unavailable\n\n==================================================\nSTEP 0: PARSE AVAILABLE JOBS DATA\n==================================================\n\nAvailable Jobs Data will be in following format:\n{\n    \"items\": [\n        {\n            \"OpportunityId\": 300100655786819,\n            \"OpportunityType\": \"REQUISITION\",\n            \"OpportunityNumber\": \"73\",\n            \"Title\": \"alert1\",\n            \"Location\": \"United States\",\n            \"PostedDate\": \"2026-06-11T12:45:20\",\n            \"RemoteOpportunityFlag\": false,\n            \"Organization\": \"Engineering\",\n            \"JobFunction\": \"\",\n            \"JobFamily\": \"\",\n            \"PrimaryWorkLocation\": \"Fusion FRCE Location 1\",\n            \"Recruiter\": \"Marian Seyes\",\n            \"HiringManager\": \"Marian Seyes\",\n            \"JobGrade\": \"\",\n            \"InternalJob\": \"true\",\n            \"Description\": \"\",\n            \"JobCode\": \"\",\n            \"ManagerLevel\": \"\",\n            \"PostingVisibility\": \"Internal and External\",\n            \"JobShift\": \"\",\n            \"FullTimePartTime\": \"\"\n        }\n\t],\n\t\"count\": 25,\n    \"hasMore\": true,\n    \"limit\": 25,\n    \"offset\": 0,\n    \"totalResults\": 978\n}\n\n- an object\n- a stringified JSON object\n- an object containing a stringified JSON response\n- an object containing output.response as a stringified JSON object\n\nFirst, parse Available Jobs Data safely.\n\nIf Available Jobs Data contains any of these string fields, parse them as JSON if possible and then search inside the parsed object:\n- output.response\n- response\n- result.response\n- body\n- data\n\nFind the job list from the first valid array among:\n- items\n- result.items\n- result.results\n- output.items\n- output.results\n- output.Results\n- parsed output.response.items\n- parsed response.items\n\nIf no valid job array exists, return exactly:\n\n{\n  \"filteredJobs\": [],\n  \"summary\": \"Available Jobs Data did not contain a valid job list.\",\n  \"status\": \"INVALID_AVAILABLE_JOBS_DATA\"\n}\n\nAll later filtering must operate only on this parsed job list.\n\n==================================================\nSTEP 1: SPECIFIC JOB NUMBER RULE\n==================================================\n\nIf resolvedSpecificJobNumber is not empty, this rule is terminal.\n\nresolvedSpecificJobNumber may contain one value or multiple comma-separated values.\n\nIf multiple Specific Job Number values are provided:\n- Evaluate each value independently.\n- Return matched jobs in the same order as the Specific Job Number values appear.\n- If the same job matches more than one value, return it only once.\n\nSearch ONLY the parsed job list for exact matches against these fields if present:\n- OpportunityId\n- OpportunityNumber\n- Title\n\nMatching rules:\n- trim spaces\n- compare case-insensitively\n- preserve exact returned job values\n- do not use partial matching\n- do not use fuzzy matching\n- do not use substring matching\n- do not use contains matching\n- match only when the normalized complete field value equals the normalized complete Specific Job Number value\n- Title matching is allowed only because Title is explicitly listed as an allowed exact-match field\n- do not match against description, responsibilities, qualifications, location, skills, or any unrelated field\n\nIf one or more exact matches are found, return:\n\n{\n  \"filteredJobs\": [matched job object or objects copied exactly from Available Jobs Data],\n  \"summary\": \"Specific job number matched from available jobs.\",\n  \"status\": \"SPECIFIC_JOB_MATCHED\"\n}\n\nIf no exact match is found, return:\n\n{\n  \"filteredJobs\": [],\n  \"summary\": \"The requested specific job number was not found in the available jobs returned by job search.\",\n  \"status\": \"SPECIFIC_JOB_NOT_FOUND\"\n}\n\nWhen resolvedSpecificJobNumber is not empty:\n- Do NOT apply Location filter\n- Do NOT apply Candidate Title filter\n- Do NOT apply Seniority Level filter\n- Do NOT apply explicit employee inclusion/exclusion filters\n- Do NOT apply skills/domain ranking\n- Stop after this rule\n\n==================================================\nSTEP 2: EXTRACT EXPLICIT EMPLOYEE INSTRUCTIONS\n==================================================\n\nRead only Referral Email Subject and Referral Email Body.\n\nIdentify explicit employee instructions.\n\nRequested inclusion examples:\n- refer only to a specific location\n- refer only to a specific title\n- refer only to a specific role\n- refer only to a specific seniority\n- refer only to a specific function/domain/category\n- refer to Director jobs\n- refer to Director jobs for India location\n- refer to Manager jobs\n- refer to QA jobs\n- refer to Java jobs\n- refer to remote jobs only\n- refer to United States jobs\n- refer to USA jobs\n- refer to suitable jobs matching the profile\n\nRequested exclusion examples:\n- do not refer to a specific location\n- exclude a specific location\n- avoid a specific location\n- not interested in a specific location\n- do not refer to a specific title\n- exclude a specific role\n- avoid a specific seniority\n- do not refer to Director jobs\n- do not refer to Manager jobs\n- do not refer to QA jobs\n\nOnly use explicit instructions.\nDo NOT infer employee intent.\nDo NOT treat generic words such as jobs, roles, openings, positions, or opportunities as specific instructions.\n\nExplicit employee instructions narrow the result set.\nExplicit employee instructions do NOT override candidate suitability unless resolvedSpecificJobNumber is present.\n\nExamples:\n- \"Refer only to Director jobs\" means keep only Director jobs that also satisfy Candidate Title and Seniority Level compatibility.\n- If no Director jobs are compatible with candidate title and seniority, return NO_FILTERED_JOBS.\n- Explicit email-requested title/role may replace a missing Candidate Title, but it must still be compatible with Seniority Level.\n- Explicit email-requested location may replace a missing Location, but excluded locations must never be used as the effective target location.\n- \"Refer the candidate to suitable jobs matching his profile\" is not a role/title override. Use resolvedCandidateTitle for title filtering.\n- \"Refer the candidate to suitable jobs matching his profile in US location\" is a positive location instruction only. Use resolvedCandidateTitle for title filtering.\n\nA job that violates an explicit exclusion instruction must be removed.\nA job that does not satisfy an explicit \"only\" instruction must be removed.\n\n==================================================\nSTEP 3: VALIDATE REQUIRED FILTER INPUTS\n==================================================\n\nBefore filtering normal jobs, validate the resolved filter inputs.\n\nIf resolvedSpecificJobNumber is empty, normal filtering requires:\n- resolvedLocation\n- resolvedCandidateTitle\n- valid resolvedSeniorityLevel\n\nIf resolvedSpecificJobNumber is empty and resolvedLocation is empty/null, return:\n\n{\n  \"filteredJobs\": [],\n  \"summary\": \"No jobs were filtered because the target location is missing.\",\n  \"status\": \"LOCATION_NOT_FOUND\"\n}\n\nIf resolvedSpecificJobNumber is empty and resolvedCandidateTitle is empty/null, return:\n\n{\n  \"filteredJobs\": [],\n  \"summary\": \"No jobs were filtered because the candidate title or explicitly requested role is missing.\",\n  \"status\": \"CANDIDATE_TITLE_NOT_FOUND\"\n}\n\nIf resolvedSpecificJobNumber is empty and resolvedSeniorityLevel is empty/null or not one of FRESHER, JUNIOR, INTERMEDIATE, SENIOR, return:\n\n{\n  \"filteredJobs\": [],\n  \"summary\": \"No jobs were filtered because the candidate seniority level is missing or invalid.\",\n  \"status\": \"SENIORITY_NOT_FOUND\"\n}\n\nImportant:\n- Do NOT return CANDIDATE_TITLE_NOT_FOUND if resolvedCandidateTitle is non-empty.\n- Do NOT return LOCATION_NOT_FOUND if resolvedLocation is non-empty.\n- Do NOT return SENIORITY_NOT_FOUND if resolvedSeniorityLevel is one of FRESHER, JUNIOR, INTERMEDIATE, SENIOR.\n- If all required resolved inputs are present, proceed to filtering. Do not stop early.\n\n==================================================\nSTEP 4: TARGET LOCATION HIERARCHY\n==================================================\n\nBuild a target location hierarchy from resolvedLocation.\n\nresolvedLocation may represent:\n- city\n- city + state/region\n- city + state/region + country\n- state/region\n- state/region + country\n- country\n\nUse only information available in:\n- resolvedLocation\n- Referral Email Subject\n- Referral Email Body\n- Candidate Resume\n- Available Jobs Data location fields\n- the explicit US city/state abbreviation rule defined in this prompt\n\nDo not derive a state or country from a city using general world knowledge, except the explicit US city/state abbreviation rule defined in this prompt.\n\nUS city/state abbreviation rule:\nIf resolvedLocation is in the format:\n<City>, <RecognizedUSStateAbbreviation>\n\nthen treat the target hierarchy as:\nCity = <City>\nState/Region = <RecognizedUSStateAbbreviation>\nCountry = United States\n\nThis rule is explicitly allowed by this prompt and is not considered external inference.\n\nExamples:\n- New Orleans, LA\n  City = New Orleans\n  State/Region = LA\n  Country = United States\n\n- Austin, TX\n  City = Austin\n  State/Region = TX\n  Country = United States\n\n- Pleasanton, CA\n  City = Pleasanton\n  State/Region = CA\n  Country = United States\n\n- Pittsburgh, PA\n  City = Pittsburgh\n  State/Region = PA\n  Country = United States\n\nWhen the US city/state abbreviation rule applies:\n- Keep jobs in the same city.\n- Keep jobs in the same state/region.\n- Keep jobs in United States.\n- Do not remove broad United States jobs only because the job location does not repeat the city or state.\n\nYou may use a city to state/country relationship only when:\n- resolvedLocation already contains city, state, and/or country; OR\n- the US city/state abbreviation rule applies; OR\n- Referral Email Subject contains the relationship; OR\n- Referral Email Body contains the relationship; OR\n- Candidate Resume contains the relationship; OR\n- Available Jobs Data contains the same city with a state/country.\n\nYou may use a state/region to country relationship only when:\n- resolvedLocation already contains state/region and country; OR\n- the US city/state abbreviation rule applies; OR\n- Referral Email Subject contains the relationship; OR\n- Referral Email Body contains the relationship; OR\n- Candidate Resume contains the relationship; OR\n- Available Jobs Data contains the same state/region with a country.\n\nIf multiple state/country mappings exist for the same city and the US city/state abbreviation rule does not apply, do not expand beyond city because the hierarchy is ambiguous.\n\nIf multiple countries map to the same state/region and the US city/state abbreviation rule does not apply, do not expand beyond state/region because the hierarchy is ambiguous.\n\nExamples:\n- resolvedLocation = \"New Orleans, LA\"\n  City = New Orleans\n  State/Region = LA\n  Country = United States\n  Keep New Orleans jobs.\n  Keep LA/Louisiana jobs.\n  Keep United States jobs.\n\n- resolvedLocation = \"New Orleans, LA, United States\"\n  City = New Orleans\n  State/Region = LA\n  Country = United States\n  Keep New Orleans jobs.\n  Keep LA/Louisiana jobs.\n  Keep United States jobs.\n\n- resolvedLocation = \"Pleasanton, CA, United States\"\n  City = Pleasanton\n  State/Region = CA\n  Country = United States\n\n- resolvedLocation = \"CA, United States\"\n  State/Region = CA\n  Country = United States\n\n- resolvedLocation = \"United States\"\n  Country = United States\n\n- resolvedLocation = \"Hyderabad\"\n  City = Hyderabad\n  State/Region and Country may be used only if explicitly paired with Hyderabad in the resume/email or Available Jobs Data.\n\nWhen retaining jobs by expanded hierarchy:\n- City match is strongest.\n- State/region match is weaker but valid.\n- Country match is broadest but valid.\n- Do not rank or score here.\n- Preserve all valid hierarchy matches for the ranking step to prioritize later.\n\n==================================================\nSTEP 5: JOB LOCATION FILTER\n==================================================\n\nFor each job in the parsed job list, identify job location only from job location fields if present, such as:\n- Location\n- PrimaryWorkLocation\n\nDo NOT use these as job location:\n- Recruiter\n- recruiter\n- HiringManager\n- hiringManager\n- company headquarters\n- employer name\n- client name\n- project geography\n- organization geography\n- global product references\n- APAC/EMEA references\n- job description mentions unrelated to posting location\n\nA job is location-aligned if it matches any allowed level of the target location hierarchy.\n\nIf resolvedLocation is a city:\n- Keep jobs in the same city.\n- Also keep jobs in the same supported state/region.\n- Also keep jobs in the same supported country.\n- City match is strongest, but state/region and country matches are still valid.\n- Only use state/region or country expansion when the hierarchy is supported by resolvedLocation, email, resume, Available Jobs Data, or the US city/state abbreviation rule.\n\nIf resolvedLocation is a state/region:\n- Keep jobs in the same state/region.\n- Also keep jobs in the same supported country.\n- State/region match is strongest, but country match is still valid.\n- Only use country expansion when the hierarchy is supported by resolvedLocation, email, resume, Available Jobs Data, or the US city/state abbreviation rule.\n\nIf resolvedLocation is a country:\n- Keep jobs in that country.\n- Remove jobs in clearly different countries unless remote/global is explicitly allowed.\n\nIf resolvedLocation is city + state/region + country:\n- Keep jobs in the city.\n- Keep jobs in the state/region.\n- Keep jobs in the country.\n\nIf resolvedLocation is state/region + country:\n- Keep jobs in the state/region.\n- Keep jobs in the country.\n\nRemote/global jobs may be retained only if:\n- the job data clearly supports remote/global hiring\nAND\n- the referral email explicitly allows remote/global jobs\n\nRemote/global signals may come from:\n- RemoteOpportunityFlag\n- Location containing Remote, Virtual, Global\n- Title containing Remote\n- Description explicitly saying remote/virtual/global\n\nRemove the job if:\n- job location clearly conflicts with the supported target hierarchy\n- job location is excluded by employee instruction\n- job location is missing and the job is not clearly remote/global with explicit permission\n- the job is in a different country and relocation/remote/global was not explicitly allowed\n\nLocation examples:\n- resolvedLocation = \"New Orleans, LA\"\n  Keep New Orleans jobs.\n  Keep LA/Louisiana jobs.\n  Keep United States jobs.\n\n- resolvedLocation = \"New Orleans, LA, United States\"\n  Keep New Orleans jobs.\n  Keep LA/Louisiana jobs.\n  Keep United States jobs.\n\n- resolvedLocation = \"Pleasanton, CA, United States\"\n  Keep Pleasanton jobs.\n  Keep CA/California jobs.\n  Keep United States jobs.\n\n- resolvedLocation = \"CA, United States\"\n  Keep CA/California jobs.\n  Keep United States jobs.\n\n- resolvedLocation = \"United States\"\n  Keep United States/US/USA jobs.\n\n- resolvedLocation = \"Hyderabad, Telangana, India\"\n  Keep Hyderabad jobs.\n  Keep Telangana jobs.\n  Keep India jobs.\n\n- resolvedLocation = \"Hyderabad\"\n  Keep Hyderabad jobs.\n  Keep Telangana or India jobs only if Hyderabad is explicitly paired with Telangana/India in the input or Available Jobs Data.\n\n==================================================\nSTEP 6: CANDIDATE TITLE / ROLE FILTER WITH NEXT-LEVEL FIT\n==================================================\n\nUse resolvedCandidateTitle and explicit employee instructions.\n\nTitle or role alignment is required.\n\nA job may pass title filtering when it has:\n- same title\n- equivalent title\n- same role family\n- realistic next-level progression\n- employee explicitly requested title/role, when still compatible with candidate seniority and role family\n\nSkill overlap alone must NOT override wrong title or unrelated role family.\n\nBefore comparing titles:\n- Ignore harmless internal/system prefixes and suffixes such as OM, ZBEN, HJ1, HJ2, Pipeline, requisition numbers, numeric suffixes, underscores, and hyphenated internal codes.\n- Do not remove real job-role words.\n- If the meaningful job title contains the full resolvedCandidateTitle, treat it as a direct or near-direct title match.\n\nExamples:\n- resolvedCandidateTitle = \"Civil Engineer\"\n  Job Title = \"OM Civil Engineer HJ2 16293619\"\n  This is a direct Civil Engineer title match.\n\n- resolvedCandidateTitle = \"Civil Engineer\"\n  Job Title = \"OM Civil Engineer Pipeline 16293254\"\n  This is a direct Civil Engineer title match.\n\n- resolvedCandidateTitle = \"Business Analyst\"\n  Job Title = \"ZBEN_JN_Business Analyst\"\n  This is a direct Business Analyst title match.\n\nIf resolvedCandidateTitle is \"FRESHER\":\nKeep jobs that are suitable for fresher or early career candidates:\n- Fresher\n- Graduate\n- Trainee\n- Intern\n- Entry Level\n- Associate\n- Junior\n- 0-1 year roles\n\nFor FRESHER candidates, next-level suitable jobs may include junior/associate jobs only when job data does not require strong prior experience.\n\nRemove jobs that are clearly too senior:\n- Intermediate\n- Senior\n- Lead\n- Principal\n- Staff\n- Architect\n- Manager\n- Director\n- Head\n- Executive\n\nIf resolvedCandidateTitle is an actual title:\nKeep jobs whose title, role family, job family, job function, or responsibilities align with the effective title/role or a realistic next-level progression.\n\nCivil Engineer role-family rule:\nIf resolvedCandidateTitle is \"Civil Engineer\" or Candidate Resume explicitly supports Civil Engineer responsibilities, keep jobs whose title contains:\n- Civil Engineer\n- OM Civil Engineer\n- Civil Engineer Pipeline\n- OM Civil Engineer Pipeline\n- Civil Engineer HJ1\n- Civil Engineer HJ2\n- OM Civil Engineer HJ1\n- OM Civil Engineer HJ2\n\nThese are direct title matches.\n\nDo not remove these Civil Engineer jobs only because:\n- Description is empty\n- Skills are empty\n- JobFunction is empty\n- JobFamily is empty\n- JobGrade is empty\n- ManagerLevel is empty\n\nCivil Engineer may also match when supported by job/resume context:\n- Structural Engineer\n- Construction Engineer\n- Site Engineer\n- Survey Engineer\n- Surveyor Engineer\n- Architecture Engineer, only if civil/architecture alignment is explicit\n\nCivil Engineer must not match:\n- Software Engineer\n- DevOps Engineer\n- Data Engineer\n- QA Engineer\n- Business Analyst\n- Sales Associate\n- Certified Nursing Assistant\n- Certified Public Accountant\n- Petroleum Engineer, unless the job explicitly says civil, infrastructure, construction, pipeline, or structural engineering in a civil-engineering sense\n- Marine Engineer, unless the job explicitly says civil, coastal, construction, structural, or infrastructure engineering\n- Retail roles\n- Nursing roles\n- Finance/accounting roles\n- Sales/business-development roles\n\nPerformance Engineering / Quality Engineering role-family rule:\nIf resolvedCandidateTitle or resume indicates:\n- Associate – Performance Engineering\n- Performance Engineer\n- Performance Test Engineer\n- Performance Testing\n- Quality Engineering Analyst\n- QA Performance Engineer\n- Quality Engineering\n\nthen keep jobs such as:\n- Performance Engineer\n- Performance Test Engineer\n- Performance Engineering Analyst\n- Quality Engineering Analyst\n- Quality Engineer\n- QA Engineer\n- Test Engineer\n- Automation Engineer\n- QA Automation Engineer\n- Software Quality Engineer\n- DevOps Engineer, only when resume explicitly contains DevOps/cloud/operations evidence such as AWS, CI/CD, Jenkins, Git, CloudWatch, Datadog, Dynatrace, monitoring, observability, scalability testing, or DevOps collaboration\n- Cloud Engineer, only when responsibilities align with cloud, performance, monitoring, scalability, reliability, or testing\n\nDo not match these profiles to unrelated jobs only because of generic words:\n- Analyst\n- Engineer\n- Developer\n- Associate\n- Data\n- Managerial\n- Principal\n\nBusiness Analyst role-family rule:\nBusiness Analyst may match:\n- Business Analyst\n- Senior Business Analyst, if seniority is compatible\n- Functional Analyst\n- Business Systems Analyst\n- Product Analyst, if responsibilities align\n- Data Analyst, if responsibilities align\n\nBusiness Analyst must not match:\n- Java Architect\n- Backend Engineer\n- QA Engineer\n- Sales Manager\n- Nurse\n- Finance Controller, unless the job is explicitly a business analyst role in finance\n\nQuality Engineering Analyst may match Senior Quality Engineer when:\n- resolvedSeniorityLevel is INTERMEDIATE or SENIOR\n- the job appears to be an individual-contributor quality engineering role\n- the candidate resume contains QA, quality engineering, testing, automation, performance testing, test strategy, or related responsibilities\n\nDo not remove the job only because the title contains \"Senior\" if the candidate is INTERMEDIATE and the role is a realistic next-level individual-contributor progression.\n\nRemove Senior Quality Engineer if:\n- it is clearly a manager, architect, principal, director, or leadership-heavy role\n- the candidate resume has no quality engineering, QA, testing, automation, or related evidence\n\nSoftware Engineer may match:\n- Software Engineer\n- Senior Software Engineer, if seniority is compatible\n- Application Developer\n- Java Developer\n- Backend Engineer\n- Full Stack Engineer, if responsibilities align\n\nSoftware Engineer must not match:\n- Business Analyst\n- Finance Analyst\n- Marketing Manager\n- Recruiter\n- Nurse\n\nQA Engineer may match:\n- QA Engineer\n- Senior QA Engineer, if seniority is compatible\n- Test Engineer\n- Automation Engineer\n- Quality Assurance Analyst\n\nQA Engineer must not match:\n- Java Architect\n- Business Analyst\n- Product Manager\n\nExamples of realistic next-level progression:\n- Civil Engineer -> Senior Civil Engineer, Structural Engineer, Construction Engineer, Site Engineer, Survey Engineer, if seniority is compatible\n- Business Analyst -> Senior Business Analyst, Functional Analyst, Product Analyst, Business Systems Analyst\n- Junior Business Analyst -> Business Analyst\n- Software Engineer -> Senior Software Engineer, Application Developer, Backend Engineer, Full Stack Engineer, Java Developer\n- Junior Software Engineer -> Software Engineer\n- QA Engineer -> Senior QA Engineer, Test Engineer, Automation Engineer, Quality Assurance Analyst\n- Data Analyst -> Senior Data Analyst, BI Analyst, Analytics Consultant\n- Consultant -> Senior Consultant\n- Developer -> Senior Developer\n- Analyst -> Senior Analyst\n\nDo not allow unrealistic jumps.\n\nExamples of unrealistic jumps:\n- Fresher -> Senior Engineer\n- Fresher -> Manager\n- Junior Analyst -> Director\n- Junior Developer -> Architect\n- Business Analyst -> Java Architect\n- Business Analyst -> QA Engineer\n- Software Engineer -> Finance Analyst\n- QA Engineer -> Product Director\n- Civil Engineer -> Software Engineer\n- Civil Engineer -> DevOps Engineer\n- Civil Engineer -> Certified Nursing Assistant\n- Civil Engineer -> Certified Public Accountant\n- IC role -> People Manager unless the resume clearly shows management/leadership experience\n\nDirector explicit instruction:\nIf the employee explicitly says to refer only to Director jobs, keep only jobs whose title or job level clearly indicates Director AND whose seniority/role fit is compatible with the candidate.\nIf the employee explicitly says not to refer to Director jobs, remove Director jobs.\n\n==================================================\nSTEP 7: SENIORITY FILTER\n==================================================\n\nUse resolvedSeniorityLevel as a mandatory filter.\n\nSeniority must be compatible with the job title, job level, manager level, years of experience, responsibilities, and leadership indicators in the job data.\n\nUse seniority signals from fields such as:\n- Title \n- ManagerLevel \n- JobGrade \n- Description \n- Qualifications \n- Responsibilities \n- InternalDescription\n- ExternalDescription\n- ExternalShortDescription\n\nInterpret resolvedSeniorityLevel as:\n\nFRESHER:\nKeep:\n- fresher\n- graduate\n- trainee\n- intern\n- entry-level\n- associate\n- junior only if clearly suitable for 0-1 year\nRemove:\n- intermediate\n- senior\n- lead\n- principal\n- staff\n- architect\n- manager\n- director\n- head\n- executive\n\nJUNIOR:\nKeep:\n- junior\n- associate\n- entry-level with some experience\n- 1-3 year jobs\n- normal non-senior role when it is a realistic next step and does not require senior ownership\nRemove:\n- senior\n- lead\n- principal\n- staff\n- architect\n- manager\n- director\n- head\n- executive\n\nINTERMEDIATE:\nKeep:\n- intermediate\n- mid-level\n- normal role title without junior/senior marker\n- analyst\n- associate, when not clearly entry-level-only\n- consultant\n- engineer\n- developer\n- specialist\n- Civil Engineer\n- OM Civil Engineer\n- OM Civil Engineer HJ1\n- OM Civil Engineer HJ2\n- OM Civil Engineer Pipeline\n- senior title only if it is a realistic next-level progression and does not clearly require 10+ years or leadership\n- jobs that appear suitable for 4-10 years\nRemove:\n- fresher-only\n- graduate-only\n- trainee-only\n- internship-only\n- clearly senior leadership roles\n- director/head/executive roles\n- principal/staff/architect roles unless the job clearly fits intermediate IC progression\n- manager roles when the candidate has no explicit people-management or leadership evidence\n\nSENIOR:\nKeep:\n- senior\n- lead\n- principal\n- staff\n- architect\n- manager\n- director\n- head\n- roles requiring 10+ years or equivalent senior responsibility\n- next-level senior progression from the candidate title when role family aligns\nRemove:\n- fresher\n- graduate\n- trainee\n- intern\n- entry-level\n- junior-only roles\n\nIf job seniority is unclear:\n- Use job title, job level, years of experience, responsibilities, and leadership indicators from the job data.\n- If still unclear, keep the job only if title and responsibilities do not conflict with resolvedSeniorityLevel.\n- Do not invent seniority.\n\nTitle progression and seniority must both be compatible.\n\nExamples:\n- resolvedCandidateTitle = Civil Engineer and resolvedSeniorityLevel = INTERMEDIATE:\n  OM Civil Engineer HJ1 may be kept.\n  OM Civil Engineer HJ2 may be kept.\n  OM Civil Engineer Pipeline may be kept.\n  Senior Civil Engineer may be kept if it appears like a realistic next-level IC role.\n  Principal Civil Engineer, Civil Engineering Manager, or Director Civil Engineer should usually be removed unless resume clearly supports principal/manager/director-level responsibility.\n\n- resolvedCandidateTitle = Associate – Performance Engineering and resolvedSeniorityLevel = INTERMEDIATE:\n  Quality Engineering Analyst may be kept.\n  Senior Quality Engineer may be kept only if it appears like a realistic IC next step.\n  Quality Engineering Manager should usually be removed unless resume clearly shows management/leadership.\n  Quality Engineering Architect should usually be removed unless resume clearly shows architecture-level ownership.\n\n- resolvedCandidateTitle = Business Analyst and resolvedSeniorityLevel = INTERMEDIATE:\n  Senior Business Analyst may be kept if it appears like a realistic next step.\n  Director Business Analyst must be removed unless email explicitly asks for Director and seniority/title evidence supports it.\n\n- resolvedCandidateTitle = Business Analyst and resolvedSeniorityLevel = JUNIOR:\n  Business Analyst may be kept as a next step.\n  Senior Business Analyst should usually be removed unless job data clearly indicates junior-friendly responsibilities.\n\n- resolvedCandidateTitle = Software Engineer and resolvedSeniorityLevel = SENIOR:\n  Senior Software Engineer, Lead Engineer, Architect may be kept if role family and responsibilities align.\n\n==================================================\nSTEP 8: APPLY EXPLICIT EMAIL REQUESTS\n==================================================\n\nApply explicit employee instructions from email subject/body after required candidate suitability filters.\n\nExamples:\n\nEmail says:\n\"Do not refer to Hyderabad jobs\"\nRemove Hyderabad jobs.\n\nEmail says:\n\"Refer only to Director jobs\"\nKeep only Director jobs that also satisfy candidate title and seniority compatibility.\n\nEmail says:\n\"Refer only to Director jobs for India location\"\nKeep only Director jobs that also satisfy candidate title and seniority compatibility along with the location match.\n\nEmail says:\n\"Refer to CA jobs but not Pleasanton\"\nKeep CA/California and United States jobs if aligned with the hierarchy, but remove Pleasanton jobs.\n\nEmail says:\n\"Refer only remote jobs\"\nKeep only jobs clearly marked remote/global/virtual.\n\nEmail says:\n\"Do not refer to QA roles\"\nRemove QA/Test roles.\n\nEmail says:\n\"Refer to Java jobs\"\nKeep only jobs whose title, skills, or responsibilities explicitly include Java, and only if the job also satisfies location, title/role, and seniority filters.\n\nEmail says:\n\"Refer the candidate to United States jobs\"\nThis is a positive location instruction only. It does not remove jobs merely because the job title is not mentioned in the email. Continue to use resolvedCandidateTitle for title filtering.\n\nEmail says:\n\"Refer the candidate to suitable jobs matching his profile\"\nThis is a generic profile-matching instruction. It does not replace resolvedCandidateTitle. Use resolvedCandidateTitle and resolvedSeniorityLevel.\n\nUse skills only to satisfy explicit employee inclusion/exclusion instructions.\nDo not use skills for general matching, ranking, scoring, or rescuing title mismatches.\n\nDo not apply implicit preferences.\nDo not over-expand terms.\n\nExamples:\n- Java does not imply Spring\n- QA does not imply automation unless explicitly present\n- Cloud does not imply AWS unless explicitly present\n- Director does not imply Manager\n\n==================================================\nSTEP 9: FINAL FILTERED JOB RETENTION\n==================================================\n\nRetain every job that satisfies all applicable normal filters:\n- Location hierarchy alignment\n- Candidate Title or realistic next-level role alignment\n- Seniority Level compatibility\n- explicit email inclusion/exclusion instructions\n\nDo NOT rank jobs.\nDo NOT limit to top 5.\nDo NOT calculate scores.\nDo NOT add explanations per job.\nReturn all filtered jobs that remain.\n\nIf no jobs remain after filtering, return:\n\n{\n  \"filteredJobs\": [],\n  \"summary\": \"No jobs remained after filtering by location hierarchy, candidate title, seniority level, and explicit referral instructions.\",\n  \"status\": \"NO_FILTERED_JOBS\"\n}\n\nIf jobs remain, return:\n\n{\n  \"filteredJobs\": [retained job objects copied from Available Jobs Data],\n  \"summary\": \"Jobs filtered by location hierarchy, candidate title, seniority level, and explicit referral instructions.\",\n  \"status\": \"FILTERED_JOBS_FOUND\"\n}\n\n==================================================\nFINAL VALIDATION\n==================================================\n\nBefore returning, verify all of the following:\n\n1. Every job in filteredJobs exists in Available Jobs Data.\n2. No job was created or invented.\n3. No job field value was modified.\n4. Specific Job Number matching used only OpportunityId/opportunityId, OpportunityNumber/opportunityNumber, RequisitionNumber/requisitionNumber, or JobCode/jobCode.\n5. If resolvedSpecificJobNumber was present, only the specific-job rule was applied.\n6. If resolvedSpecificJobNumber was absent, resolvedLocation, resolvedCandidateTitle, and resolvedSeniorityLevel were applied.\n7. If resolvedCandidateTitle was non-empty, CANDIDATE_TITLE_NOT_FOUND was not returned.\n8. If resolvedLocation was non-empty, LOCATION_NOT_FOUND was not returned.\n9. If resolvedSeniorityLevel was valid, SENIORITY_NOT_FOUND was not returned.\n10. If resolvedLocation was city-level with a recognized US state abbreviation, the country United States was added to the supported hierarchy.\n11. If resolvedLocation was New Orleans, LA, United States jobs were allowed as country-level matches.\n12. If resolvedLocation was city-level, city, supported state/region, and supported country matches were allowed.\n13. If resolvedLocation was state/region-level, state/region and supported country matches were allowed.\n14. No unsupported location hierarchy was invented.\n15. Realistic next-level title progression was allowed.\n16. Civil Engineer jobs were retained when resolvedCandidateTitle was Civil Engineer, location was aligned, and seniority was compatible.\n17. OM Civil Engineer HJ1, OM Civil Engineer HJ2, and OM Civil Engineer Pipeline were treated as Civil Engineer title matches.\n18. Unrealistic title/seniority jumps were removed.\n19. Explicit employee exclusions were honored.\n20. Explicit employee \"only\" instructions were honored.\n21. Explicit employee instructions narrowed results and did not override suitability.\n22. No ranking or scoring was performed.\n23. No matchScore, matchedSkills, missingSkills, or justification fields were added.\n24. Output is exactly one valid JSON object.\n25. Output starts with { and ends with }.\n\nReturn only raw JSON."
            }
          ],
